Guide.content._register("w03", {"id":"w03","order":3,"region":"knowledge","title":"Data Ingestion","short":"Ingest the data","tagline":"The answer is not in the system yet. Get it in, keep it true, and know when it is wrong.","story":"The question has crossed the model and the cache, and it needs knowledge the system does not hold yet. Files sit in storage, rows change in a database, pages live in a library with permissions, and some of it is scanned paper. Ingestion decides what the assistant can ever know, how stale it is, and whether a deleted or restricted document keeps answering. This world is the choice of pipeline and the discipline of keeping the index equal to the source.","oneLiner":"Choose how content gets in, parse it faithfully, keep it fresh, repeatable and deletable, and gate what enters the index.","youWillLearn":["Pick an ingestion architecture (indexer, push, event-driven, batch) from source mix, freshness and team size","Choose a parser by document type, volume and residency, and represent tables and figures so they stay answerable","Detect changes and deletes per source, and say what each indexer and change feed cannot see","Make writes idempotent with stable ids and content hashes so retries and replays are safe","Re-embed with zero downtime using a new index behind an alias, with a rollback window","Design dead-letter, retry and backpressure paths, and gate PII and permissions at ingest","Know which Azure AI Search ingestion features are GA and which are preview as of 2026-10-13"],"minutes":85,"prereqs":["w02"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w03-fc-001","front":"What are the smallest and longest Azure AI Search indexer schedule intervals?","back":"5 minutes to 1,440 minutes (24 hours), set as an XSD duration such as `PT2H`. For seconds of freshness, push from an event pipeline instead of scheduling an indexer.","tags":["indexer"]},{"id":"w03-fc-002","front":"What are the push indexing batch limits and the meaning of HTTP 207 and 503?","back":"At most 1,000 documents or 16 MB per request (HTTP 413 above). 207 means partial failure, so check each document. 503 means wait and retry.","tags":["push"]},{"id":"w03-fc-003","front":"Why is `mergeOrUpload` the usual push action, and why is replay safe?","back":"It upserts by key, so a replayed batch overwrites instead of duplicating. `delete` of a key that is not in the index is also safe.","tags":["push","idempotency"]},{"id":"w03-fc-004","front":"What do the Blob and Cosmos DB indexers do with hard deletes?","back":"They never see them. You need a soft-delete policy from the first run (native blob soft delete, or a flag such as `IsDeleted=true`), or a reconciliation job.","tags":["deletes"]},{"id":"w03-fc-005","front":"Which Azure SQL change detection policy sees deleted rows?","back":"`SqlIntegratedChangeTrackingPolicy`, for tables only. `HighWaterMarkChangeDetectionPolicy` (rowversion or updated column) also works for views, and deletes then need a soft-delete column policy.","tags":["change-detection"]},{"id":"w03-fc-006","front":"What does the Cosmos DB change feed in latest version mode miss?","back":"Deletes, and every intermediate update: it keeps only the latest create or update per item. All versions and deletes mode records everything but needs continuous backup and reads only within that window.","tags":["cdc"]},{"id":"w03-fc-007","front":"Event Grid and Debezium both deliver at least once. What follows?","back":"Duplicates will happen after faults, so the sink must be idempotent: stable keys, upserts and a hash check. Exactly-once effect comes from the write, not the pipe.","tags":["cdc","idempotency"]},{"id":"w03-fc-008","front":"How do you fire a blob event only after a block blob is fully committed?","back":"Filter `BlobCreated` on the `CopyBlob`, `PutBlob`, `PutBlockList` or `FlushWithClose` API calls, so a worker never reads a half-written blob.","tags":["events"]},{"id":"w03-fc-009","front":"Why does a restored blob or a renamed ADLS Gen2 directory stay out of the index?","back":"Change detection reads the blob `LastModified` timestamp, and neither a restore nor a directory rename changes it. Touch the blob or reset the document or indexer.","tags":["change-detection","gotcha"]},{"id":"w03-fc-010","front":"What are the PII Detection skill defaults, and how do you mask?","back":"`maskingMode` defaults to `none` and `minimumPrecision` to 0.0. Set `maskingMode: replace` to get `maskedText`. Run it before embedding if PII must not reach the index.","tags":["pii"]},{"id":"w03-fc-011","front":"What must projections copy so ACLs can be enforced at query time?","back":"The ACL fields (for example `metadata_user_ids`, `metadata_group_ids`) onto every chunk. Knowledge source `ingestionPermissionOptions` needs `2026-08-01-preview`, not `2026-04-01`.","tags":["acl","preview"]},{"id":"w03-fc-012","front":"How do you re-embed a corpus with zero downtime?","back":"Build a new index with the new model beside the old one, validate it, repoint the index alias, and keep the old index for a rollback window. Embedding models cannot be upgraded in place.","tags":["alias","reembedding"]},{"id":"w03-fc-013","front":"What does `maxFailedItems: -1` do and why is it risky?","back":"The indexer keeps going however many documents fail. Poison documents then vanish quietly, so watch error counts and keep a dead-letter list.","tags":["errors"]},{"id":"w03-fc-014","front":"What is the Document Layout skill timeout and billing quirk?","back":"Documents that need over 5 minutes in the layout model time out, and are still charged when a Foundry resource is attached. Split very large files first.","tags":["parsing"]},{"id":"w03-fc-015","front":"What changed in Document Intelligence layout v4.0 (2024-11-30) that breaks old parsers?","back":"Tables come out as HTML tables, and selection marks are the characters ☒ and ☐ instead of `:selected:` and `:unselected:`. Re-run your parsing tests on upgrade.","tags":["parsing"]},{"id":"w03-fc-016","front":"When does a vision LLM beat OCR or layout analysis, and what does it cost?","back":"On charts and visual pages where the meaning is in the picture. It costs tokens per page, is not deterministic, and can drop or invent cells, so validate on a labelled set.","tags":["parsing","vision"]},{"id":"w03-fc-017","front":"Why do child chunk keys from index projections replace stale chunks, and what do deletes need?","back":"Projected keys contain a hash that changes when the parent changes. Children of a deleted parent go only if the data source has a deletion detection policy.","tags":["projections","deletes"]},{"id":"w03-t01-fc1","front":"What are the smallest and largest indexer schedule intervals?","back":"5 minutes and 1,440 minutes (24 hours), set as an XSD duration such as `PT2H`.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t01"},{"id":"w03-t01-fc2","front":"Push API batch limits and status codes?","back":"1,000 documents or 16 MB per request. 413 is too large, 207 is partial failure, 503 means wait and retry.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t01"},{"id":"w03-t01-fc3","front":"Why must event-driven workers be idempotent?","back":"Event Grid and Debezium deliver at least once, so the same change can arrive twice. The sink must tolerate replays.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t01"},{"id":"w03-t02-fc1","front":"Which Azure AI Search indexer data sources are GA and which are preview?","back":"GA: Blob (incl. ADLS Gen2), Cosmos DB NoSQL, Azure SQL, SQL MI, Table, OneLake. Preview: SharePoint, MySQL, Cosmos Mongo and Gremlin. Cassandra unsupported.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t02"},{"id":"w03-t02-fc2","front":"How do Blob and Cosmos DB indexers learn about deletes?","back":"Only through a soft-delete signal set up before the first run: blob soft delete or a metadata flag, and a Boolean field in Cosmos DB.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t02"},{"id":"w03-t02-fc3","front":"What should a crawler do on 429 or 503 from Graph or SharePoint?","back":"Wait the `Retry-After` seconds, reduce concurrency and spread bursts. Immediate retries count against limits.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t02"},{"id":"w03-t03-fc1","front":"How do you get Markdown from Document Intelligence layout?","back":"Set `outputContentFormat=markdown` (v4.0 `2024-11-30`, GA). Tables come as HTML tables and selection marks as ☒ and ☐.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t03"},{"id":"w03-t03-fc2","front":"Document Layout skill: output formats and the timeout?","back":"`outputFormat` is `markdown` (default) or `text`. Documents needing over 5 minutes in the layout model time out.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t03"},{"id":"w03-t03-fc3","front":"When does a local parser such as Docling beat a cloud layout service?","back":"When data cannot leave your network or volume makes per-page pricing hard. You then own compute, upgrades and tuning.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t03"},{"id":"w03-t04-fc1","front":"Why can vision-based parsing cause re-embedding churn?","back":"Small run-to-run text differences change the content hash. Cache parses by file hash, parser version and prompt version.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t04"},{"id":"w03-t04-fc2","front":"Content Understanding skill chunking options?","back":"`method`: `fixedSize` (default) or `semantic` (preview). `maximumLength` default 500, `overlapLength` below half, `fixedSize` only.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t04"},{"id":"w03-t04-fc3","front":"What does Content Understanding add over a plain prompt?","back":"Schema-defined fields with confidence scores and grounding across documents, images, audio and video.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t04"},{"id":"w03-t05-fc1","front":"How does layout v4.0 represent tables and checkboxes?","back":"Tables as HTML tables with merged cells and multi-row headers. Selection marks as the characters ☒ and ☐.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t05"},{"id":"w03-t05-fc2","front":"Why repeat the header in every table chunk?","back":"A chunk without headers has values with no column meaning, so retrieval and the model can pair values with the wrong labels.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t05"},{"id":"w03-t05-fc3","front":"When should a table go to a structured store?","back":"When questions need sums, ranks, filters or joins. Embeddings of cell text cannot compute them reliably.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t05"},{"id":"w03-t06-fc1","front":"Why is a content hash a useful gate in incremental ingestion?","back":"It skips items that were touched but not changed, so parse and embedding cost follow real edits, not timestamps.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t06"},{"id":"w03-t06-fc2","front":"SQL indexer: integrated change tracking versus high-water mark?","back":"Change tracking: tables only, sees deletes, recommended. High-water mark: rowversion or last-updated column, works for views, needs a soft-delete column for deletes.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t06"},{"id":"w03-t06-fc3","front":"Which indexer reset is GA, and which are preview?","back":"Reset Indexer is GA. Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync are preview APIs in `2026-08-01-preview`.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t06"},{"id":"w03-t07-fc1","front":"Event Grid delivery guarantees and defaults?","back":"At least once, no ordering guarantee. Defaults: 30 attempts and 1,440 minute event TTL. Dead-letter only if configured.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t07"},{"id":"w03-t07-fc2","front":"What is effectively-once processing?","back":"At-least-once delivery combined with an idempotent sink, such as a stable key and `mergeOrUpload`.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t07"},{"id":"w03-t07-fc3","front":"Why re-read the source on each change event?","back":"Events can be duplicated or out of order. Indexing current state makes stale events harmless.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t07"},{"id":"w03-t08-fc1","front":"What makes a replayed push batch safe?","back":"`mergeOrUpload` with stable keys, and `delete` on a missing key being safe. The same batch lands on the same documents.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t08"},{"id":"w03-t08-fc2","front":"Why use an immutable source id for the document key?","back":"Paths change on rename or move, which would orphan old chunks and create duplicates.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t08"},{"id":"w03-t08-fc3","front":"Why include the embedding model version in a chunk-hash cache key?","back":"A stored vector is only valid for its model. A different model needs new vectors even for identical text.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t08"},{"id":"w03-t09-fc1","front":"Why can't you upgrade an embedding model in place?","back":"Vectors from different models are not comparable. Azure says you need new embeddings, so build a new index and switch an alias.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t09"},{"id":"w03-t09-fc2","front":"Two soft-delete strategies for the Blob indexer?","back":"Native blob soft delete (versioning off, long retention) or a metadata flag like `IsDeleted=true` then physical delete. Policy must exist from the first run.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t09"},{"id":"w03-t09-fc3","front":"What must match between the embedding skill and the index at query time?","back":"The vectorizer's model must be the same as the embedding skill's model that produced the stored vectors.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t09"},{"id":"w03-t10-fc1","front":"Service Bus DLQ default max delivery count and reason code?","back":"Default 10. The reason is `MaxDeliveryCountExceeded`. Nothing cleans the DLQ automatically.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t10"},{"id":"w03-t10-fc2","front":"Embeddings request limits on Azure OpenAI?","back":"2,048 inputs and 300,000 tokens per request, summed across inputs. Compute both limits when batching.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t10"},{"id":"w03-t10-fc3","front":"Why can a 429 appear below your token quota?","back":"Bursts can exceed rate limits even when usage metrics look fine. Smooth traffic with a limiter and backoff.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t10"},{"id":"w03-t11-fc1","front":"Why must PII masking happen before embedding?","back":"After embedding, the vector and stored text already carry the data. Removing it later means rebuilding the index.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t11"},{"id":"w03-t11-fc2","front":"Which settings does ACL ingestion need in Azure AI Search knowledge sources?","back":"`ingestionPermissionOptions` (such as `userIds`, `groupIds`) on `2026-08-01-preview`, plus projection mappings copying ACL fields to every chunk.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t11"},{"id":"w03-t11-fc3","front":"Name four metadata fields worth stamping on each chunk.","back":"Source id, page or region, content hash and parser or embedding model version. Also language, sensitivity label and chunk position.","tags":[],"topic":"w03-t11"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Indexer overview - Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-indexer-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Which sources have GA and preview indexers, and what an indexer and skillset do. The page was last updated 2025-06-23, so confirm each source page.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Load data into a search index (push and pull)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-load-search-index","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Index Documents actions, the 1,000 documents or 16 MB batch cap and the 207, 413 and 503 responses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Schedule indexers in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-howto-schedule-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The 5 minute floor and 24 hour ceiling for indexer schedules.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Change and delete detection using indexers for Azure Storage","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-azure-blob-changed-deleted","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"LastModified detection, soft-delete strategies and the restore and rename traps.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r5","title":"Index data from Azure SQL Database","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-sql-database","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Integrated change tracking, high water mark policies and soft-delete column detection.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Index data from Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-cosmosdb-sql","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The _ts high water mark and why hard deletes are invisible to the indexer.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Index data from SharePoint in Microsoft 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-sharepoint-online","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Preview indexer: registration, 75 minute token expiry, unsupported encrypted files and delete detection.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r8","title":"Run or reset indexers, skills and documents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-howto-run-reset-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Reset Indexer (GA) and the Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync preview APIs for targeted replay.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r9","title":"Create an index alias in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-alias","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Repoint an alias to cut over to a rebuilt index with no application change.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Define index projections in a skillset","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-define-index-projections","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"One-to-many chunk indexing, projectionMode, key behaviour, deletes and ACL field mapping.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"Integrated vectorization in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-integrated-vectorization","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Indexer, skillset and vectorizer as one pipeline, and the same-model rule for query-time vectorizers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r12","title":"Knowledge sources overview (Foundry IQ and Azure AI Search)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-knowledge-source-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Indexed versus remote knowledge sources and which kinds carry a preview label. Mixed GA and preview, so check per kind.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r13","title":"Create a blob knowledge source","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-knowledge-source-how-to-blob","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"ingestionPermissionOptions and ADLS Gen2 ACL ingestion, which need 2026-08-01-preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r14","title":"PII Detection skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-pii-detection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"minimumPrecision, maskingMode, maskingCharacter, domain and piiCategories.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"Document layout analysis - Document Intelligence","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/document-intelligence/prebuilt/layout","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"v4.0 (2024-11-30) Markdown output, HTML tables, selection marks, handwriting flags and figures.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Document Layout skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-document-intelligence-layout","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Output formats, markdownHeaderDepth, chunkingProperties, the 5 minute timeout and billing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"What is Azure Content Understanding?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"API 2025-11-01 (GA) and the 2026-06-01-preview additions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Azure Content Understanding skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-content-understanding","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"GA in 2026-04-01: extract and chunk in one skill. Semantic chunking and AI image descriptions are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"Docling","url":"https://github.com/docling-project/docling","type":"repo","publisher":"Docling project","note":"MIT-licensed document parsing that can run locally, with layout, tables and OCR.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"Chunking - Unstructured open source","url":"https://docs.unstructured.io/open-source/core-functionality/chunking","type":"doc","publisher":"Unstructured","note":"max_characters, new_after_n_chars and overlap defaults, and the basic and by_title strategies.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"OmniDocBench: Benchmarking Diverse PDF Document Parsing with Comprehensive Annotations","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.07626","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Compares pipeline parsers with vision-language models across nine document sources.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"ColPali: Efficient Document Retrieval with Vision Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01449","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Embeds page images directly as multi-vector embeddings, skipping text parsing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Change feed modes in Azure Cosmos DB","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/change-feed-modes","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What latest version and all versions and deletes modes record, and their limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"Reacting to Blob storage events","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blob-event-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Event Grid delivery, retry, dead-lettering and the at-least-once caveat.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r25","title":"Debezium connector for PostgreSQL","url":"https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/postgresql.html","type":"doc","publisher":"Debezium","note":"Log-based CDC, at-least-once delivery, delete events and tombstones.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Azure AI Search service limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-limits-quotas-capacity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Indexer run time caps and push request limits by tier.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Document Intelligence service quotas and limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/document-intelligence/service-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"File size and page limits per tier.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"Content Understanding service limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/service-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Document size and page caps for asynchronous analysis.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r29","title":"Extract data with Azure Document Intelligence (Learn module)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/extract-data-with-document-intelligence/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Hands-on study material for layout extraction and prebuilt models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r30","title":"Implement advanced RAG pipelines with Azure AI Search and Microsoft Foundry (Learn module)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/aaai-implement-advanced-rag-azure-ai-search/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Study material for an end-to-end ingestion and retrieval pipeline.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Preprocessing Unstructured Data for LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/preprocessing-unstructured-data-for-llm-applications","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Short course on parsing and normalising messy documents before chunking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r32","title":"Index data from Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-azure-data-lake-storage","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"maxFailedItems, maxFailedItemsPerBatch and failOnUnsupportedContentType error tolerance settings.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r33","title":"Monitor indexer status and results","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-monitor-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Run status, per-run errors and warnings, execution history and what Success hides.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r34","title":"Foundry Models sold directly by Azure (embedding models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Embedding input limits, up to 2,048 inputs per call, and no in-place upgrade between embedding models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r35","title":"Develop a RAG solution: chunking phase (Azure Architecture Center)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-chunking-phase","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Chunking approaches by document structure, and repeating an image URL when its description splits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Indexer overview in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-indexer-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What an indexer does, which data sources are GA or preview, and how skillsets attach (page last updated 2025-06-23, confirm per source page).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Load data into a search index (push with the Index Documents API)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-load-search-index","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Push actions, the 1,000 document or 16 MB batch cap, and how 207, 413 and 503 behave.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Schedule indexers in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-howto-schedule-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The 5 minute floor and 1,440 minute ceiling for indexer schedules.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Change and delete detection using indexers for Azure Storage","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-index-azure-blob-changed-deleted","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"LastModified change detection, the two soft-delete strategies and the restore and rename traps.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Index data from Azure SQL Database","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-index-sql-database","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Integrated change tracking, high-water-mark policy and soft-delete column policy.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Index data from Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-index-cosmosdb-sql","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The `_ts` high-water mark and the Boolean soft-delete field requirement.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Index data from SharePoint in Microsoft 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-index-sharepoint-online","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Preview indexer, token expiry, supported content and the ACL and label previews.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Index data from Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-index-azure-data-lake-storage","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Error tolerance with `maxFailedItems` and `maxFailedItemsPerBatch`, and unsupported content types.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr9","title":"Run or reset indexers, skills and documents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-howto-run-reset-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Reset Indexer (GA) and the preview Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync APIs for replay.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Add an index alias in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-alias","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Aliases as service-level names you repoint for blue-green cutovers, and the tier limitation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr11","title":"Define index projections (parent-child indexing)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-define-index-projections","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"One-to-many chunk indexing, projection modes, key behaviour and delete handling.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr12","title":"Integrated vectorization in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/vector-search-integrated-vectorization","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Indexer, skillset and vectorizer pipeline, and the rule that query and index embeddings use the same model.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr13","title":"Knowledge sources in Foundry IQ and Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/agentic-knowledge-source-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Indexed versus remote knowledge source kinds and their preview status.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr14","title":"Create a blob knowledge source (permission ingestion)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/agentic-knowledge-source-how-to-blob","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"`ingestionPermissionOptions` and the ACL fields copied onto each chunk, preview API only.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr15","title":"PII Detection skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-pii-detection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Skill parameters `minimumPrecision`, `maskingMode`, `piiCategories` and `domain`.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr16","title":"Document layout analysis in Document Intelligence","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/document-intelligence/prebuilt/layout","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Markdown output, HTML tables, selection marks, handwriting flags and figure regions in v4.0 `2024-11-30`.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr17","title":"Document Layout skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-document-intelligence-layout","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Skill parameters, the 5 minute timeout and billing beyond 20 documents per indexer per day.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr18","title":"What is Azure Content Understanding","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"GA `2025-11-01`, the `2026-06-01-preview` additions and schema-defined field extraction.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr19","title":"Content Understanding skill","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/cognitive-search-skill-content-understanding","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Extract and chunk in one skill, chunking properties, semantic chunking and image descriptions in preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr20","title":"Docling","url":"https://github.com/docling-project/docling","type":"repo","publisher":"Docling project","note":"MIT-licensed local document parser for PDFs and Office files.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr21","title":"Unstructured chunking","url":"https://docs.unstructured.io/open-source/core-functionality/chunking","type":"doc","publisher":"Unstructured","note":"`max_characters`, `new_after_n_chars`, `overlap` defaults and the `by_title` strategy.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr22","title":"OmniDocBench: Benchmarking Diverse PDF Document Parsing","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.07626","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Parsing quality varies by document type, comparing pipelines and vision-language models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr23","title":"Change feed modes in Azure Cosmos DB","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cosmos-db/change-feed-modes","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Latest version versus all versions and deletes mode, and what each captures.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr24","title":"Reacting to Blob Storage events","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blob-event-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Event Grid delivery, retries and the API filters that fire `BlobCreated` after a full commit.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr25","title":"Debezium connector for PostgreSQL","url":"https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/postgresql.html","type":"doc","publisher":"Debezium","note":"Log-based CDC, at-least-once delivery, delete events and tombstones.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr26","title":"Event Grid delivery and retry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/event-grid/delivery-and-retry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"At-least-once delivery, no ordering guarantee, retry schedule, 30 attempt and 1,440 minute defaults, dead-lettering.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr27","title":"Service Bus dead-letter queues","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/service-bus-messaging/service-bus-dead-letter-queues","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Default max delivery count of 10, `MaxDeliveryCountExceeded`, and no automatic DLQ cleanup.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr28","title":"Azure OpenAI quotas and limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/quotas-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"2,048 inputs and 300,000 tokens per embeddings request, subscription-level quota and 429 guidance.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr29","title":"Microsoft Graph throttling guidance","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/graph/throttling","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"429 with `Retry-After` and why immediate retries make throttling worse.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr30","title":"Avoid getting throttled or blocked in SharePoint Online","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/sharepoint/dev/general-development/how-to-avoid-getting-throttled-or-blocked-in-sharepoint-online","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"429 and 503 with `Retry-After`, reducing concurrency and decorating traffic.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr31","title":"Service limits, quotas and capacity in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-limits-quotas-capacity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Push batch size, indexer run time and index limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr32","title":"Azure OpenAI model retirement schedule","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirement-schedule","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Embedding model retirement dates (page updated 2026-09-21), check the live schedule before quoting.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr33","title":"Foundry Models sold directly by Azure","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Embedding model table: input limit, dimensions, 2,048 inputs per call, no in-place upgrade between models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr34","title":"Document Intelligence service limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/document-intelligence/service-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"File size and page limits per tier.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr35","title":"Content Understanding service limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/service-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Document size and page limits for asynchronous analysis.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr36","title":"Extract data with Azure Document Intelligence","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/extract-data-with-document-intelligence/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Hands-on module for document extraction (catalog updated 2026-06-11).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr37","title":"Implement advanced RAG pipelines with Azure AI Search and Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/aaai-implement-advanced-rag-azure-ai-search/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"End-to-end ingestion and retrieval module (catalog updated 2026-08-04).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr38","title":"Preprocessing Unstructured Data for LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/preprocessing-unstructured-data-for-llm-applications","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Short course on parsing and chunking messy documents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr39","title":"ColPali: Efficient Document Retrieval with Vision Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01449","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Embeds page images as multi-vector embeddings so retrieval can skip text parsing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr40","title":"Personally identifiable information detection in Azure AI Language","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/language-service/personally-identifiable-information/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Text, conversation and document-based PII modes, categories and confidence scores.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr41","title":"Vector index size and staying under limits in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/vector-search-index-size","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The vector index size quota is a memory quota: HNSW graphs are held in memory.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Indexer","def":"An Azure AI Search crawler that pulls from a supported data source on a schedule, detects changes and can run a skillset on the way into an index."},{"term":"Skillset","def":"The enrichment pipeline an indexer runs per document: parse, OCR, split, embed or call a custom skill."},{"term":"Push model","def":"Your code sends documents to the index with the Index Documents API (`upload`, `merge`, `mergeOrUpload`, `delete`). You own parsing, chunking and embedding."},{"term":"Change data capture","def":"Reading inserts, updates and deletes from a database log or change feed so downstream systems receive each change instead of rescanning the table."},{"term":"Idempotent write","def":"A write that gives the same result when repeated. With stable keys and `mergeOrUpload`, a replayed batch overwrites rather than duplicates."},{"term":"Content hash","def":"A digest of a document or chunk's text, stored beside it. If the hash is unchanged, skip parsing, embedding and writing."},{"term":"Soft delete","def":"Marking a document as deleted (a flag or the storage service's own soft delete) so an indexer can see the delete, then removing it from the index."},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A record that says an item was deleted. Change streams emit tombstones so consumers can remove the item downstream."},{"term":"Dead-letter queue","def":"A holding place for messages or documents that failed after retries, kept with the failure reason so they can be inspected and replayed."},{"term":"Backpressure","def":"A signal or limit that slows producers when a consumer or downstream API (such as an embedding endpoint) cannot keep up."},{"term":"Index alias","def":"A service-level secondary name for an index. Repointing it moves traffic to a rebuilt index with no application change."},{"term":"Blue-green index","def":"Run the old (blue) and new (green) index side by side, validate green, switch the alias, and keep blue for rollback."},{"term":"Index projection","def":"A skillset feature that writes one index document per chunk (one to many) with a parent key, so chunks can be filtered and replaced by parent."},{"term":"Layout analysis","def":"A parsing model that returns reading order, tables, headings, figures and page coordinates, not only a text stream."},{"term":"Content Understanding","def":"An Azure service that analyses documents, images, audio and video into schema-defined fields with confidence scores, also exposed as an AI Search skill."},{"term":"PII Detection skill","def":"An AI Search skill that finds personal data in text and can return a masked copy before the text is embedded."},{"term":"Indexer","def":"An Azure AI Search component that pulls from a supported data source on a schedule, detects changes and can run a skillset before writing to an index."},{"term":"Push ingestion","def":"Your code sends documents to the index with the Index Documents API using `upload`, `merge`, `mergeOrUpload` or `delete`."},{"term":"Change data capture (CDC)","def":"Reading a database's change log to emit an event for every insert, update and delete."},{"term":"At-least-once delivery","def":"A messaging guarantee where an event is delivered one or more times, so consumers must tolerate duplicates."},{"term":"Idempotent write","def":"A write that gives the same final state when repeated, usually because the key is derived from the source and the action is an upsert."},{"term":"Content hash","def":"A digest of the normalised document or chunk text, used to skip unchanged content and to find duplicates."},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A record that marks an item as deleted so downstream stores can remove it, such as a soft-delete flag or a null-valued delete event."},{"term":"Index alias","def":"A service-level name that points to an index. Repointing it switches an application to a rebuilt index with no code change."},{"term":"Dead-letter queue (DLQ)","def":"A side queue for messages that could not be processed, kept for inspection, repair and replay."},{"term":"Backpressure","def":"A signal or mechanism that slows producers when a downstream stage such as the embedding API cannot keep up."},{"term":"Layout analysis","def":"Detecting reading order, headings, tables, figures and selection marks on a page rather than extracting raw text only."},{"term":"Content Understanding","def":"An Azure service that analyses documents, images, audio and video into schema-defined fields with confidence scores and grounding."},{"term":"Index projection","def":"A skillset feature that writes one index document per chunk, linked to its parent by a filterable parent id field."},{"term":"Blue-green index","def":"Building a second index beside the live one, validating it, then switching an alias so rollback stays possible."},{"term":"ACL capture","def":"Copying a source's permission data onto every chunk at ingest so retrieval can trim results to what the caller may read."}],"levels":[{"id":"w03-l1","order":1,"title":"Pick the pipe","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"Files, rows and pages must become searchable chunks. Nobody has told you the freshness target, the source mix or who carries the pager. Choose how content gets in, and say what would flip your choice.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five ways in, three dials","level":2,"id":"five-ways-in-three-dials"},{"t":"p","md":"Every ingestion design answers the same three questions: **how fresh** must the index be, **what kind of sources** feed it, and **who owns the pipeline** when it breaks. The mechanisms are in topics `w03-t01` (architectures), `w03-t02` (connectors) and `w03-t07` (CDC and events). This level makes you choose between them. A scheduled indexer whose skillset also chunks and embeds is called integrated vectorization [[r11]]. Start with the numbers that decide most of it."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Indexer schedule floor","value":"5 min","note":"Longest interval is 1,440 minutes (24 hours) [[r3]]"},{"label":"Push batch cap","value":"1,000 docs or 16 MB","note":"HTTP 413 above it, 207 on partial failure, 503 means retry [[r2]]"},{"label":"Indexer run time to plan for","value":"about 2 h","note":"The limits page lists 2 or 24 hours by tier [[r26]]"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w03-l1-arch","title":"Pick an ingestion architecture","prompt":"Flip the three constraints. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"mix","label":"Source mix","values":[{"id":"ga","label":"Mostly GA stores (Blob, SQL, Cosmos DB, OneLake)"},{"id":"odd","label":"SharePoint, other apps or no indexer"}]},{"id":"rate","label":"Freshness target","values":[{"id":"hours","label":"Hours or daily"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"}]},{"id":"team","label":"Scale and ownership","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Modest corpus, small team"},{"id":"big","label":"Huge corpus, platform team on call"}]}],"options":[{"id":"indexer","name":"Scheduled indexer with skillset","summary":"Azure AI Search pulls from the source, detects changes and runs built-in skills."},{"id":"push","name":"Push from your own code","summary":"A service reads the source, chunks, embeds and calls the Index Documents API."},{"id":"event","name":"Event-driven pipeline, then push","summary":"Blob events, a change feed or CDC feed a queue and workers that push."},{"id":"batch","name":"Batch ETL and rebuild","summary":"A scheduled job re-reads and rebuilds large parts of the index."},{"id":"foundry","name":"Foundry IQ knowledge source","summary":"Declare a knowledge source and let it generate the indexer pipeline."}],"scores":{"indexer":{"mix":{"ga":{"s":2,"why":"Blob, ADLS Gen2, Cosmos DB, Azure SQL, Table Storage and OneLake have GA indexers with built-in change detection [[r1]]."},"odd":{"s":-1,"why":"The SharePoint indexer is preview and some sources have no indexer, so you may still need a push path [[r1]]."}},"rate":{"hours":{"s":2,"why":"A schedule can be as sparse as every 1,440 minutes [[r3]]."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"The floor is 5 minutes, and staleness is the interval plus the run time, which can reach hours [[r26]]."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing schedules faster than 5 minutes [[r3]]."}},"team":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Declarative: no workers, queues or retry code to own."},"big":{"s":-1,"why":"Run time is capped (plan for 2 hours), so a big backfill spans many runs, and you cannot tune retries or parsing per file [[r26]]."}}},"push":{"mix":{"ga":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you rebuild the change detection the indexer gives for free."},"odd":{"s":2,"why":"Any source your code can read can be pushed, through any parser you choose."}},"rate":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you must write and run the scheduler."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Call the API when your trigger fires, with no 5 minute floor."},"seconds":{"s":1,"why":"Latency is yours to control, but you still need a trigger that says what changed."}},"team":{"small":{"s":-1,"why":"You own parsing, chunking, embedding, retries and monitoring."},"big":{"s":1,"why":"You control batching (1,000 documents or 16 MB per request), parallelism and throttling [[r2]]."}}},"event":{"mix":{"ga":{"s":0,"why":"Blob events and the Cosmos DB change feed exist, but you build the glue [[r23]] [[r24]]."},"odd":{"s":1,"why":"Log-based CDC such as Debezium reaches databases that have no indexer, with at-least-once delivery [[r25]]."}},"rate":{"hours":{"s":-1,"why":"Overbuilt: queues and workers for freshness nobody asked for."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Reacts to each change instead of polling."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"The only option here that reacts per change, if the workers keep up."}},"team":{"small":{"s":-2,"why":"The queue, workers, dead letters and replay all need an owner and an on-call rota."},"big":{"s":2,"why":"Workers scale on their own, with dead letters and replay under your control."}}},"batch":{"mix":{"ga":{"s":0,"why":"Simple to reason about, but it ignores the incremental detection the indexers provide."},"odd":{"s":1,"why":"ETL tools reach almost any source in bulk."}},"rate":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"A nightly rebuild is fine when a day of staleness is acceptable."},"minutes":{"s":-2,"why":"A rebuild takes longer than the freshness target."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"A rebuild cannot meet seconds."}},"team":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"One scheduled job is easy to own."},"big":{"s":-2,"why":"Re-embedding unchanged content every night wastes embedding calls at scale."}}},"foundry":{"mix":{"ga":{"s":1,"why":"The indexed kinds for a search index, Blob and OneLake carry no preview label [[r12]]."},"odd":{"s":-1,"why":"Azure SQL, File and Indexed SharePoint kinds are preview, and remote kinds query live instead of ingesting [[r12]]."}},"rate":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"It generates an indexer pipeline, so schedules apply [[r12]]."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Expect the indexer schedule floor and run caps to apply, since the pipeline is an indexer [[r12]]."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"An indexer pipeline cannot refresh in seconds."}},"team":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Least code: choose a source and the pipeline is generated."},"big":{"s":0,"why":"You give up per-file control of parsing and retries."}}}},"takeaway":"Freshness decides first, because the indexer floor is 5 minutes. The source mix decides second, because indexers cover some stores well and others not at all. Team size decides last, and it is the dial people forget."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-l1-decide","title":"A decision path you can say aloud","alt":"A decision tree. If seconds of freshness are needed, use an event-driven push. Otherwise, if the source has no GA indexer or built-in skills are not enough, push from your own code. Otherwise use a scheduled indexer, and add a batch or push backfill if the backlog is too big for the run caps.","src":"flowchart TD\n  A{Seconds of freshness?} -->|yes| E[Event-driven push]\n  A -->|no| B{GA indexer for the source?}\n  B -->|no| P[Push from your code]\n  B -->|yes| C{Built-in skills enough?}\n  C -->|no| P\n  C -->|yes| I[Scheduled indexer]\n  I --> D{Backfill too big for run caps?}\n  D -->|yes| K[Add a push backfill]\n  D -->|no| OK[Keep the indexer]","caption":"Mixing paths is normal: the indexer for the long tail, a push path for the few sources where seconds matter."},{"t":"failure","title":"How ingestion goes wrong","items":[{"symptom":"The index lags the source by much more than the schedule says","cause":"Staleness is the interval plus the run time. A backlog or slow skills can fill a capped run [[r26]].","fix":"Chart documents per run and run duration. Split the corpus across several data sources or move the bulk to a push backfill."},{"symptom":"A retried queue message writes a document twice","cause":"Event Grid and Debezium deliver at least once, so duplicates follow any fault [[r24]] [[r25]].","fix":"Upsert with a stable key (`mergeOrUpload`) and skip the write when the content hash is unchanged."},{"symptom":"A push batch is rejected, or looks fine yet some documents are missing","cause":"A request above 1,000 documents or 16 MB returns 413, and HTTP 207 means only some documents failed [[r2]].","fix":"Cap batches by bytes as well as count, read the per-document status on 207, retry only the failures, and back off on 503."},{"symptom":"The indexer says Success but documents are missing","cause":"A run succeeds while errors stay below Max failed items [[r33]].","fix":"Alert on per-run error counts and the documents-succeeded trend, not on the run status alone."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview status as of 2026-10-13","md":"The SharePoint in Microsoft 365 indexer needs a preview REST API [[r7]]. Foundry IQ indexed kinds for Azure SQL, File and Indexed SharePoint are preview, while search index, Blob and OneLake carry no preview label [[r12]]. Index projections and the Content Understanding skill are GA in REST `2026-04-01`, while Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync need `2026-08-01-preview` [[r8]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"A support portal keeps articles as blobs, and an edited article must be searchable within 30 seconds. The indexer currently runs every 5 minutes. What change fits?","options":["Set the indexer schedule to every 30 seconds so the next run picks up the edit","Move to a bigger search tier so each indexer run finishes much faster","Publish blob events to a queue and let workers parse, embed and push the article","Replace the indexer with a batch job that rebuilds the whole index every 5 minutes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The schedule floor is 5 minutes, so no setting reaches 30 seconds, and a bigger tier only shortens the run, not the interval. A rebuild every 5 minutes is slower still and wastes embedding calls. Events trigger work per change, and the workers must be idempotent because delivery is at least once [[r3]] [[r24]]."},{"id":"w03-l1-q2","type":"multi","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"A push worker times out and resends the same batch. Select every fact that makes the replay safe or helps keep it safe.","options":["`mergeOrUpload` on a stable key overwrites the document instead of duplicating it","`delete` of a key that is not in the index does not fail","HTTP 207 means nothing was written, so a retry must resend every document","Document keys come from the source id plus chunk position, not a random GUID per attempt"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Upserting by key and tolerating deletes of missing keys make the batch repeatable, and a deterministic key is what lets the upsert find the earlier copy. A random GUID per attempt would create duplicates. HTTP 207 means partial failure, so resending only the failed documents is correct, and resending everything is merely wasteful [[r2]]."},{"id":"w03-l1-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"You backfill 2,400,000 chunk documents by push. Each document is about 40 KB as JSON including its vector. Taking 16 MB as 16,000 KB, what is the minimum number of requests?","answer":6000,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"requests","hint":"Each request is limited by both the 1,000 document cap and the 16 MB cap. Which one binds?","explain":"16,000 KB divided by 40 KB gives 400 documents per request, below the 1,000 document cap, so bytes bind. 2,400,000 / 400 = 6,000 requests. Using the document cap alone gives 2,400, the usual mistake [[r2]]."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r7","r8","r11","r12","r23","r24","r25","r26","r33","r30"],"takeaways":["Choose in this order: freshness, source mix, then who owns the pipeline","The indexer floor is 5 minutes and a run can last hours, so seconds of freshness mean events and push","Event Grid and Debezium deliver at least once, so every sink must be idempotent","Cap push batches by bytes and count, and alert on error counts because Success can hide failures"]},{"id":"w03-l2","order":2,"title":"Parse it right or pay for it later","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"Half of the corpus is clean exports, the rest is scans with merged-cell tables, forms and charts. One parser for everything is tempting. Pick by document type, volume and where the text may travel.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What the parser must hand over","level":2,"id":"what-the-parser-must-hand-over"},{"t":"p","md":"Chunking cannot repair what parsing lost. A parser has to return reading order, tables with their structure, figures and, when you cite pages, coordinates. Topics `w03-t03` (extraction, OCR, layout), `w03-t04` (vision LLMs and Content Understanding) and `w03-t05` (tables, figures, forms) explain the mechanisms. Benchmarks agree that quality varies by document type [[r21]], and some systems skip text parsing and embed page images instead [[r22]]. Here you choose per document class."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Document Layout skill timeout","value":"5 min per document","note":"Still charged when a Foundry resource is attached [[r16]]"},{"label":"Document Intelligence S0 file limit","value":"500 MB, 2,000 pages","note":"The free F0 tier allows 4 MB and 2 pages [[r27]]"},{"label":"Content Understanding async PDF limit","value":"200 MB, 300 pages","note":"Checked 2026-10-13 [[r28]]"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w03-l2-parser","title":"Pick a parser for a document class","prompt":"Flip the constraints. Real corpora need a different answer per class, so run the board once for each.","dials":[{"id":"doc","label":"Document type","values":[{"id":"clean","label":"Clean born-digital text"},{"id":"scans","label":"Scans, tables and forms"},{"id":"visual","label":"Charts, diagrams, visual pages"}]},{"id":"vol","label":"Volume","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Thousands of pages"},{"id":"huge","label":"Hundreds of millions of pages"}]},{"id":"where","label":"Where text may go","values":[{"id":"cloud","label":"A managed cloud service is allowed"},{"id":"local","label":"Text must stay in our network"}]}],"options":[{"id":"native","name":"Native text extraction","summary":"Read the text layer of born-digital files, with no model involved."},{"id":"layout","name":"Layout analysis (Document Intelligence)","summary":"A managed OCR and layout model that returns Markdown, tables, figures and coordinates."},{"id":"cu","name":"Content Understanding","summary":"Managed analyzers that extract and chunk in one skill, with schema-defined fields."},{"id":"oss","name":"Open-source parser you run","summary":"Docling or Unstructured with layout, tables and OCR, self-hosted."},{"id":"vlm","name":"Vision LLM reads each page","summary":"Send page images to a multimodal model and ask for structured text."}],"scores":{"native":{"doc":{"clean":{"s":2,"why":"The text layer is already there, so extraction is fast, cheap and deterministic."},"scans":{"s":-2,"why":"A scan has no text layer, and flattened tables lose their structure."},"visual":{"s":-2,"why":"Charts and figures carry no extractable text, so their content is lost."}},"vol":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap at any size, though a small volume does not need cheap."},"huge":{"s":2,"why":"No per-page fee and no model throughput limit."}},"where":{"cloud":{"s":0,"why":"No effect: it runs anywhere."},"local":{"s":2,"why":"A local library keeps the text inside your network."}}},"layout":{"doc":{"clean":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you pay for a model that adds little to clean text."},"scans":{"s":2,"why":"OCR, per-line handwriting flags and HTML tables with merged cells and multi-row headers, in Markdown output [[r15]]."},"visual":{"s":1,"why":"Figures come back with bounding regions, not a description of what they show [[r15]]."}},"vol":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"A per-page price is a small bill at this volume."},"huge":{"s":-1,"why":"Per-page cost and service limits add up (S0 allows 500 MB and 2,000 pages per file) [[r27]]."}},"where":{"cloud":{"s":1,"why":"Managed, and v4.0 (2024-11-30) is GA [[r15]]."},"local":{"s":-2,"why":"It is a cloud service. Confirm a deployment that keeps data in your network before assuming one."}}},"cu":{"doc":{"clean":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but it is more machinery than clean text needs."},"scans":{"s":1,"why":"Layout-aware extraction. The AI Search skill outputs tables as Markdown and lets chunks span pages [[r18]]."},"visual":{"s":2,"why":"Covers images, audio and video, and AI image descriptions exist, though they are preview [[r17]] [[r18]]."}},"vol":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"Fine at small volume."},"huge":{"s":-1,"why":"Asynchronous document analysis is capped at 200 MB and 300 pages per PDF, so large files need splitting [[r28]]."}},"where":{"cloud":{"s":1,"why":"API 2025-11-01 is GA, and the skill is GA in search REST 2026-04-01 [[r17]] [[r18]]."},"local":{"s":-2,"why":"It is a managed cloud service, so text leaves your network."}}},"oss":{"doc":{"clean":{"s":1,"why":"Reads PDF, DOCX, PPTX, XLSX and HTML without a service call [[r19]]."},"scans":{"s":1,"why":"Docling covers layout, reading order, tables, code, formulas and OCR for scans [[r19]]."},"visual":{"s":0,"why":"Finding a figure is not describing it. A chart description needs a vision model on top."}},"vol":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"You run and patch the service even for a small job."},"huge":{"s":1,"why":"No per-page fee, so cost is compute and operations, which you can scale."}},"where":{"cloud":{"s":0,"why":"Allowed, but not a reason to pick it."},"local":{"s":2,"why":"It can run locally for sensitive or air-gapped data. The Docling code is MIT licensed, and model licences vary [[r19]]."}}},"vlm":{"doc":{"clean":{"s":-1,"why":"Slower and costlier than reading the text layer, and the output can drift between runs."},"scans":{"s":1,"why":"Reads messy layouts well, but can drop or invent cells, so every table needs validation."},"visual":{"s":2,"why":"The one option here that can read a chart or diagram and say what it shows."}},"vol":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"Token cost per page is tolerable at small volume."},"huge":{"s":-2,"why":"Token cost per page and model throughput dominate at this volume."}},"where":{"cloud":{"s":0,"why":"Allowed."},"local":{"s":-1,"why":"Only a self-hosted vision model qualifies, which adds GPU operations."}}}},"takeaway":"No parser wins every class. Route by document type: native text for clean files, layout analysis or a local parser for scans and tables, and a vision model only for the pages whose meaning is a picture."},{"t":"table","caption":"Keep tables, figures and forms answerable","head":["Content","Representation","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Table","Whole table as HTML or Markdown in one chunk, with its caption","It fits in a chunk and questions need several rows","A large table splits mid-row or exceeds the embedding input limit"],["Table","One chunk per row, with the header repeated in each","Questions look up one row, such as a price or a limit","Questions compare many rows, and the index grows by the row count"],["Table","A short LLM summary embedded, raw table kept in the payload","Natural-language questions about a dense numeric table","The summary drops a number, so the raw table must reach the model"],["Figure","Caption plus a vision-model description as its own chunk, with the image URL","The answer lives in a chart or diagram","The description is a model guess. If it splits across chunks, repeat the image URL in each chunk [[r35]]"],["Form","Extract named fields into a schema and store them as metadata","Questions ask for a field, such as an invoice total","Layouts vary so much that field confidence is low, so route those to review [[r17]]"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"failure","title":"Parsing failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Checkbox and table parsing code broke after an upgrade to layout v4.0","cause":"v4.0 (2024-11-30) renders tables as HTML and selection marks as the characters ☒ and ☐, instead of `:selected:` and `:unselected:` [[r15]].","fix":"Parse the new forms and keep a regression set of tricky pages that runs on every parser upgrade."},{"symptom":"Big PDFs fail in the Document Layout skill yet still show on the bill","cause":"Documents needing over 5 minutes in the layout model time out, and are still charged when a Foundry resource is attached [[r16]].","fix":"Split very large files into page ranges before ingest, or route them through a separate batch path."},{"symptom":"Answers quote the wrong number from a table","cause":"A merged-cell or multi-row header was flattened, or a vision model dropped a cell.","fix":"Score tables against a labelled set per document type, and keep the raw table so the model reads cells instead of a summary."},{"symptom":"Every document re-embeds after a parser upgrade","cause":"Open-source parsers release often (Docling v2.138.0 on 2026-10-12, Unstructured 0.27.25 on 2026-10-10) and the output shifts [[r19]] [[r20]].","fix":"Pin versions, run the regression set before upgrading, and include the parser version in the re-embedding key beside the content hash."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Where the docs disagree, as of 2026-10-13","md":"The Content Understanding skill page says it outputs tables and figures as Markdown and that the Document Layout skill gives plain text, while the Document Layout skill page lists `markdown` as its default `outputFormat` [[r16]] [[r18]]. Test both on your own tables. Semantic chunking in the Content Understanding skill and AI image descriptions are preview (`2026-05-01-preview`), and API `2026-06-01-preview` adds signature detection and document metadata extraction [[r17]] [[r18]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"Questions about a merged-cell price table return wrong numbers. The pipeline extracts the text layer and splits recursively, and the chunks read cleanly. What do you change first?","options":["Switch to a parser that keeps table structure, then re-test the table questions","Raise top-k from 5 to 20 so that more of the table's rows are retrieved per query","Move to a larger embedding model so that numbers and units embed more precisely","Increase the chunk overlap so that each row of the table appears in two chunks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Text-layer extraction flattens merged cells and multi-row headers before any chunker sees them, so the numbers already lost their column. Layout analysis returns tables as HTML with merged cells preserved [[r15]]. A higher top-k, a bigger embedding model and more overlap all operate on the flattened text and cannot restore the structure."},{"id":"w03-l2-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"You propose sending every page of a 100-million-page archive to a vision LLM. Select the costs a reviewer should raise.","options":["Token cost per page scales with volume and dominates the bill","Output is not deterministic, so a re-run or model update can change the chunks","It can never run inside a private network, even with a self-hosted model","Cells can be dropped or invented, so tables need validation against a labelled set"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Per-page token cost, non-deterministic output and silent cell errors are the real costs, and they push you to route only visual pages to the model. A self-hosted vision model can run on a private network, at the price of GPU operations, so the third option overstates the limit."},{"id":"w03-l2-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each parsing component to the fact most likely to bite you.","pairs":[{"l":"Document Layout skill","r":"Times out when a document needs over 5 minutes in the layout model"},{"l":"Layout v4.0 (2024-11-30)","r":"Tables as HTML and selection marks as ☒ and ☐"},{"l":"Content Understanding skill","r":"GA in search REST 2026-04-01, extracts and chunks in one skill"},{"l":"Docling","r":"MIT-licensed code that can run locally on air-gapped data"}],"explain":"Each pairing is a documented constraint you would plan around: the skill timeout and billing [[r16]], the v4.0 output change that breaks old parsers [[r15]], the single extract-and-chunk skill [[r18]] and the local, MIT-licensed option [[r19]]."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r15","r16","r17","r18","r19","r20","r21","r22","r27","r28","r29","r31","r35"],"takeaways":["Chunking cannot repair what parsing lost, so test tables and figures before you tune chunk size","Route by document class: native text, layout analysis or a local parser, and a vision model only where the picture is the content","Name the version and status: layout v4.0 changed table and checkbox output, and Content Understanding semantic chunking is preview","Keep a regression set of hard pages and re-run it on every parser upgrade"]},{"id":"w03-l3","order":3,"title":"Changed, repeated, deleted","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Documents change, events arrive twice, sources delete files without telling anyone, and the embedding model will change next year. Make the index tell the truth about the source, and say how you would prove it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Detect: what each source can and cannot tell you","level":2,"id":"detect-what-each-source-can-and-cannot-tell-you"},{"t":"p","md":"An index is only as true as its change and delete signals. Topics `w03-t06` (incremental ingestion), `w03-t07` (CDC and events), `w03-t08` (idempotency and hashing) and `w03-t09` (deletes, versioning, re-embedding) give the mechanisms. The matrix below is the part interviewers probe: for each source, what is seen, what is invisible and what breaks silently."},{"t":"table","caption":"Change and delete signals by source","head":["Source and mode","Change signal","Delete signal","Trap"],"rows":[["Blob and ADLS Gen2 indexer","Blob `LastModified`, built in","None by default. Native blob soft delete (blob versioning off) or a metadata flag such as `IsDeleted=true`, then a physical delete","The policy must exist from the first run. A restored blob or a renamed ADLS directory is not re-indexed because `LastModified` does not change [[r4]]"],["Azure SQL indexer","`SqlIntegratedChangeTrackingPolicy` (tables only) or `HighWaterMarkChangeDetectionPolicy` on a `rowversion` or updated column","Change tracking sees deleted rows. Otherwise `SoftDeleteColumnDeletionDetectionPolicy` with `softDeleteColumnName`","Integrated change tracking is for tables, so a view needs the high water mark policy [[r5]]"],["Cosmos DB for NoSQL indexer","`HighWaterMarkChangeDetectionPolicy` on `_ts`","A top-level Boolean soft-delete field such as `IsDeleted` and a `dataDeletionDetectionPolicy`","Hard deletes are never seen [[r6]]"],["Cosmos DB change feed, latest version","Creates and updates, only the latest change per item","Not captured. Use a soft-delete flag plus TTL","No intermediate changes, no filter by operation type, no fixed retention [[r23]]"],["Cosmos DB change feed, all versions and deletes","Every create, update and delete in order, with change-type metadata","Captured, including TTL deletes","Needs continuous backup and an opt-in flag, reads only inside the backup retention window, starts only at the current time or a checkpoint [[r23]]"],["SharePoint indexer (preview)","The next scheduled run","Deleted files, list items and pages are detected on the next run","Preview. User-delegated tokens expire every 75 minutes, and password-protected files are not supported [[r7]]"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"h","text":"Repeat and swap: safe writes and zero-downtime re-embedding","level":2,"id":"repeat-and-swap-safe-writes-and-zero-downtime-re-embedding"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"An idempotent ingest step (sketch, not a specific SDK)","code":"def ingest(doc, state, index):\n    # re-embed only when text, parser, chunker or embedding model changes\n    fp = sha256(f\"{doc.text}|{PARSER_V}|{CHUNKER_V}|{EMBED_MODEL}\".encode()).hexdigest()\n    if state.get(doc.id) == fp:\n        return                                  # unchanged: no embedding cost\n    rows = [\n        {\"id\": f\"{doc.id}-{i}\", \"parent_id\": doc.id, \"content_hash\": fp,\n         \"content\": c, \"vector\": embed(c)}\n        for i, c in enumerate(split(doc.text))\n    ]\n    index.merge_or_upload(rows)                 # replay-safe upsert by key\n    index.delete_where(parent_id=doc.id, id_not_in=[r[\"id\"] for r in rows])  # stale tail chunks\n    state[doc.id] = fp                          # written last, so a crash replays the document","caption":"The ids come from the source id and chunk position, the fingerprint includes everything that changes the output, and the state write comes last."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-l3-bluegreen","title":"Blue-green index behind an alias","alt":"The application queries an alias that points at the old index. A pipeline fills a new index with the new embedding model. After a recall comparison on labelled queries passes, the alias is repointed to the new index, and the old index is kept for a rollback window.","src":"flowchart LR\n  APP[Application] --> AL[Alias]\n  AL --> BLUE[Index v1 old model]\n  PIPE[Re-embed pipeline] --> GREEN[Index v2 new model]\n  GREEN --> EVAL[Compare recall on labelled queries]\n  EVAL -->|pass| SW[Repoint alias to v2]\n  SW --> AL\n  BLUE --> KEEP[Keep for rollback window]","caption":"Vectors from different models are not comparable and Azure OpenAI embeddings cannot be upgraded in place, so a new index with new vector fields is the safe path [[r34]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Freshness and deletion failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"A deleted file still answers questions","cause":"Blob and Cosmos DB indexers do not see hard deletes, and the policy must exist from the first run [[r4]] [[r6]].","fix":"Enable a soft-delete policy, remove the orphans by key, and run a reconciliation job that lists source ids and compares them with index keys."},{"symptom":"A restored file or renamed folder never comes back","cause":"`LastModified` does not change on a restore or an ADLS Gen2 directory rename [[r4]].","fix":"Touch the blob or reset the affected documents, and put restore and rename in your delete tests."},{"symptom":"Chunks of a deleted parent linger in the index","cause":"Index projections remove child chunks only when the data source has a deletion detection policy [[r10]].","fix":"Add the policy, then alert on chunks whose `parent_id` is missing from the source."},{"symptom":"An old version overwrites a newer one","cause":"Delivery is at least once and not ordered: items in one Cosmos DB transactional batch share a modification time and may arrive in any order [[r23]] [[r24]] [[r25]].","fix":"Carry a version or modified time on each event and write only when it is newer than the stored one."},{"symptom":"Search returns nonsense right after an embedding model change","cause":"New queries are embedded by one model and old vectors by another.","fix":"Build the new index beside the old one and cut over by alias, never edit vectors in place."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Replay controls, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Reset Indexer is GA and re-runs everything. Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync are preview APIs in `2026-08-01-preview` [[r8]]. An alias is a service-level resource, and the Serverless Developer tier does not support aliases [[r9]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"A blob indexer has run nightly for months. A user deleted a policy PDF last week, yet the assistant still cites it. No deletion detection policy was ever set. What is the right fix?","options":["Run Reset Indexer, which will notice that the blob is gone and remove its chunks","Shorten the schedule to every 5 minutes so that the delete is seen much sooner","Raise `maxFailedItems` so that the missing blob stops being counted as a failure","Delete the orphan by key, turn on soft delete, and reconcile ids with the index"],"answer":3,"explain":"The indexer cannot see a hard delete at any schedule, and a reset re-reads only blobs that still exist, so the orphan stays. The policy has to exist before the delete happens, and a reconciliation job is the safety net for anything the policy misses. `maxFailedItems` governs failures, not missing sources [[r4]]."},{"id":"w03-l3-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the steps of a zero-downtime embedding model migration in order.","items":["Create a new index with vector fields sized for the new model","Re-embed the corpus into it while the old index keeps serving","Run the labelled queries on both and compare recall","Repoint the alias to the new index","Keep the old index for a rollback window, then delete it"],"explain":"The new index must exist before it can be filled, and it is validated before any user sees it. The alias switch is the instant cutover, and the old index is your rollback until the window passes. Because the two models' vectors cannot be mixed, there is no in-place shortcut [[r9]] [[r34]]."},{"id":"w03-l3-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"You feed a pipeline from the Cosmos DB change feed. Select the statements that are true.","options":["Latest version mode does not capture deletes, so use a soft-delete flag plus TTL","All versions and deletes mode can be read only within the continuous backup retention window","Latest version mode records every intermediate update of an item","Items in one transactional batch share a modification time and may arrive in any order"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Latest version mode keeps only the most recent change per item and misses deletes, all versions and deletes mode needs continuous backup and has a retention window, and batch items are unordered. So the third option is false, and the consumer must tolerate gaps and reordering [[r23]]."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r4","r5","r6","r7","r8","r9","r10","r23","r24","r25","r34"],"takeaways":["Know the delete story per source: Blob and Cosmos DB indexers need a soft-delete policy from day one, and hard deletes need reconciliation","Make every write idempotent: stable ids, a fingerprint that includes parser, chunker and model, and a state write last","At-least-once delivery means duplicates and reordering, so carry a version and write only newer data","Re-embed into a new index and cut over by alias, and keep the old one for a rollback window"]},{"id":"w03-l4","order":4,"title":"Poison, pressure and permissions","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"One corrupt PDF blocks the queue, the embedding API starts returning 429, and compliance asks whether a national id number can reach the index and who may read it. Decide what the pipeline does at each gate.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"When a document fails, the pipeline must not","level":2,"id":"when-a-document-fails-the-pipeline-must-not"},{"t":"p","md":"A pipeline that handles the happy path is a demo. Topics `w03-t10` (dead letters, backpressure, replay) and `w03-t11` (PII, ACL capture, metadata, observability) give the mechanisms. This level is about classification: which failures retry, which go to a dead-letter store, and which gates must run **before** a chunk is embedded, because a vector cannot be un-leaked."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-l4-deadletter","title":"Retry, throttle or dead-letter","alt":"Messages flow from a queue to a worker. Success writes to the index. A transient error goes to a retry with backoff and back to the worker. A poison document, or one whose retries are spent, goes to a dead-letter store with its reason, then to triage and back to the queue once fixed. The worker calls an embedding API, and a rate limit response tells it to slow down.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Queue] --> W[Worker]\n  W -->|ok| IDX[Index]\n  W -->|transient| R[Retry with backoff]\n  R --> W\n  W -->|poison or retries spent| DLQ[Dead-letter store with reason]\n  DLQ --> T[Triage and fix]\n  T -->|replay| Q\n  W -->|embed call| EMB[Embedding API]\n  EMB -->|429 slow down| W","caption":"Backpressure is the arrow from the embedding API back to the worker: concurrency follows the rate limit, not the queue depth."},{"t":"table","caption":"Classify the failure before you choose the action","head":["Failure class","Examples","Action"],"rows":[["Transient","429 from the embedding API, HTTP 503 from search","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter, and lower worker concurrency. The search docs say wait and retry on 503 [[r2]]"],["Partial batch failure","HTTP 207 on a push batch","Read the per-document status and retry only the failed documents [[r2]]"],["Oversized request","A push request above 1,000 documents or 16 MB (HTTP 413), or an embeddings call with more than 2,048 inputs","Split by bytes and count, then retry. Azure embeddings accept up to 2,048 inputs per call [[r2]] [[r34]]"],["Poison document","A corrupt file, an unsupported type, a password-protected or user-encrypted file","Dead-letter on the first deterministic failure, with the reason. Never retry forever [[r7]]"],["Tolerated by configuration","`maxFailedItems` and `maxFailedItemsPerBatch`, where `-1` continues however many errors occur","Use a finite limit so a bad deploy stops the run, and alert on the count [[r32]]"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"flow","title":"Quality gates before the vector is written","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"Capture permissions","md":"Copy ACL fields such as `metadata_user_ids`, `metadata_group_ids` and `metadata_spo_site_url` onto every chunk through index projection mappings [[r13]].","tag":"acl"},{"title":"Detect and mask PII","md":"The PII skill takes `minimumPrecision` (0.0 to 1.0, default 0.0), `maskingMode` (default `none`, `replace` returns `maskedText`), `maskingCharacter`, `domain` and `piiCategories`. Run it before embedding if PII must not reach the index [[r14]].","tag":"pii"},{"title":"Enrich metadata","md":"Title, section path, source URL, modified time, content hash, parser and chunker version, and sensitivity label, so you can filter and debug later.","tag":"metadata"},{"title":"Embed and write","md":"Idempotent upsert by stable key, with the state record written last."},{"title":"Observe","md":"Per-document status, stage latency, dead-letter depth and a count of source ids against index keys. A run can report Success while documents are missing [[r33]].","tag":"ops"}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes at the gates","items":[{"symptom":"Queue depth climbs and errors rise after the embedding API returns 429","cause":"Workers retry immediately with no backoff and no concurrency cap, so retries add load to a service that is already limiting you.","fix":"Exponential backoff with jitter, concurrency tied to the rate limit, and a pause when 429s persist."},{"symptom":"One bad PDF holds up the whole queue","cause":"There is no dead-letter path, so the same message retries forever.","fix":"Cap attempts, then move the message to a dead-letter store with the reason and the file hash. Replay it after the fix."},{"symptom":"A run reports Success, yet about 4 percent of documents never arrive","cause":"A high or `-1` failure tolerance lets a run succeed while errors stay below the limit [[r32]] [[r33]].","fix":"Set a finite limit, alert on the error count and its trend, and publish a dead-letter report."},{"symptom":"Chunks are returned to people who should not see them","cause":"ACLs were captured on the parent only, or not at all. `ingestionPermissionOptions` needs `2026-08-01-preview`, and `2026-04-01` does not support it [[r13]].","fix":"Map the ACL fields onto every chunk, test with a restricted user, and plan a re-sync for permission changes. Query-time trimming belongs to the security chapter."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Status as of 2026-10-13","md":"ACL ingestion for ADLS Gen2 and SharePoint is preview, and the SharePoint indexer itself is preview [[r7]] [[r13]]. The PII Detection skill is GA [[r14]]. Treat any permission feature you adopt as a dependency with an end date to check."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"The embedding API returns 429, queue depth is growing, the error rate is climbing and every worker retries immediately. What is the right fix?","options":["Add more workers so that the backlog drains before the limit takes effect","Cap concurrency to the rate limit and retry with exponential backoff and jitter","Retry up to ten times at once, then drop the message if it still fails","Switch the queue to at-most-once delivery so that the retries stop completely and quickly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Immediate retries multiply the load on a service that is already limiting you. Backoff with jitter spreads retries and a concurrency cap keeps you under the limit. More workers make the storm worse, dropping messages loses documents, and at-most-once delivery trades a retry problem for silent loss."},{"id":"w03-l4-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"Permissions must be enforceable later at query time. Select what has to be true at ingest.","options":["The system prompt tells the model to refuse content a user should not see","ACL or group ids are copied onto every chunk, not only the parent document","A re-sync path exists, because permissions change without the content changing","On API `2026-08-01-preview` the knowledge source sets `ingestionPermissionOptions`, since `2026-04-01` does not support it"],"answers":[1,2,3],"explain":"A query-time filter needs the permission data on each chunk it filters, and a re-sync path keeps it true after a permission change. As of 2026-10-13 knowledge source ACL ingestion is a preview feature. A prompt instruction is not access control, because the content has already reached the model [[r13]]."},{"id":"w03-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":3,"q":"An HR assistant must let analysts ask for an employee's national id, while all other staff must never see it. How should PII be handled at ingest?","options":["Mask every PII value with `maskingMode: replace` before it is embedded","Drop every document that contains PII from the corpus entirely","Keep the value, put ACLs on each chunk and filter by the caller's groups","Index masked text and keep the original values in an application log"],"answer":2,"explain":"Masking before embedding is right when no answer ever needs the value, but here it destroys the analysts' use case, and dropping documents does too. Logging the original does not make it retrievable. Keeping the value with per-chunk permissions satisfies both groups, provided the filter is enforced before ranking [[r14]]."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r7","r13","r14","r32","r33","r34"],"takeaways":["Classify failures: retry transient ones with backoff, retry only the failed documents of a 207, and dead-letter poison documents with a reason","Backpressure means concurrency follows the downstream rate limit, not the queue depth","Gate PII and permissions before embedding, because a vector cannot be un-leaked","A Success status can hide errors, so alert on error counts and source to index drift"]},{"id":"w03-l5","order":5,"title":"Boss: the mixed, changing corpus","kind":"boss","minutes":18,"brief":"An interviewer describes four kinds of source, content that changes all day and permissions that differ per document. No numbers are given. Design the ingestion layer and defend it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Your four minutes","level":2,"id":"your-four-minutes"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you are handed","known":["Scanned and born-digital PDFs in blob storage","A SharePoint library, rows in a product database and a wiki","Content changes all day, and permissions differ per document","The interviewer will probe deletes, replay, cost and what happens when permissions change"],"ask":["How fresh must each source be, and which source matters most?","How fast must a delete or a permission revocation take effect?","How many documents and pages, and what share is scanned?","May text leave the network for managed parsing and embedding?","Who owns the pipeline on call, and what does a wrong answer cost?"],"tension":"Seconds of freshness and exact deletes cost operations effort, while managed indexers are simple but slower and not available for every source."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-l5-stack","title":"A defensible hybrid, as a starting point","alt":"Blob files and a SharePoint library feed a scheduled indexer. The product database and the wiki feed a CDC path to a queue and workers that parse and embed. Both paths write to a green index behind an alias. Workers send failures to a dead-letter store, and a reconciliation job compares source ids with index keys.","src":"flowchart LR\n  B[Blob files] --> IX[Scheduled indexer]\n  SP[SharePoint library] --> IX\n  DB[Product database] --> CD[CDC to queue]\n  WK[Wiki] --> CD\n  CD --> W[Workers parse and embed]\n  IX --> G[Index behind alias]\n  W --> G\n  W --> DL[Dead-letter store]\n  RC[Reconciliation job] --> G","caption":"Each box has a dial that can remove or replace it. SharePoint indexing is preview as of 2026-10-13."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-l5-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"The index holds 50 million chunks and about 2 percent of them change each day. By what factor does an incremental pipeline embed fewer chunks per day than a nightly full rebuild?","answer":50,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"times fewer","hint":"Compare 100 percent of the corpus with the changed share.","explain":"A rebuild embeds all 50 million chunks, and the incremental path embeds about 1 million. 100 / 2 = 50. The factor shrinks as churn rises, so measure the real change rate, and note that a changed document can touch every chunk it owns [[r4]]."},{"id":"w03-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":3,"q":"A new rule says a deleted document must stop appearing within 5 minutes. Which change fits best?","options":["Shorten every indexer schedule to 5 minutes and keep the current delete settings","Send tombstone events, delete chunks by parent id, and reconcile ids nightly","Rebuild the whole index into a fresh index every 5 minutes, then swap the alias","Tell the model in the prompt to ignore documents that are marked as deleted"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indexer floor is 5 minutes before the run time is added, and a blob or Cosmos DB indexer sees only soft deletes anyway. Events can remove chunks as soon as the delete is known, and reconciliation catches the ones that were missed. A rebuild every 5 minutes is impractical, and a prompt instruction leaves the content in the index [[r3]] [[r4]] [[r6]]."},{"id":"w03-l5-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":3,"q":"A dashboard shows the index holds exactly as many documents as the source, yet users report stale and missing answers. What does the matching count fail to prove?","options":["That the embedding model is the same for every chunk in the index","That the permissions on each chunk are correct for every user","That the chunk size is right for the question types asked","That the same ids are present and the content hashes match"],"answer":3,"explain":"Counts can match while a deleted document lingers and a new one never arrived, and updates that were never applied leave the count untouched. Compare the set of ids and the content fingerprints per document. The other options are also unproven by a count, but none of them explain stale and missing answers at equal counts [[r33]]."},{"id":"w03-l5-q4","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"Select the signals worth alerting on for this pipeline.","options":["Age of the oldest message in the dead-letter store","The indexer run status alone, because Success means every document arrived","Drift: ids in the source but not the index, and ids in the index but not the source","The 429 rate from the embedding API and the queue depth"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Dead-letter age shows poison documents nobody has triaged, drift shows silent loss and orphans, and 429 rate with queue depth shows backpressure before it becomes lag. A run can succeed while errors stay below the failure tolerance, so status alone hides problems [[r33]]."}],"defend":{"prompt":"We have scanned and born-digital PDFs in blob storage, a SharePoint library, product rows in a database and a wiki. Everything changes during the day, deleted content must stop appearing quickly and access differs per document. Design the ingestion layer and tell me how you would know it is correct.","timebox":"4 min","clarify":["How fresh must each source be, and which one is the strictest?","How fast must a delete or a permission revocation take effect?","How many documents and pages are there, and what share is scanned or table-heavy?","May text leave our network for managed parsing and embedding?","Who owns the pipeline on call, and what does a wrong or leaked answer cost?","Which embedding model will we use, and do we expect to change it?"],"options":[{"name":"Managed indexers and skillsets for every source","when":"Sources are GA stores, minutes of staleness is fine and built-in skills cover the parsing.","tradeoff":"5 minute schedule floor, run caps (plan for 2 hours), SharePoint is preview, and you cannot apply your own retry and dead-letter policy per file."},{"name":"Event-driven pipeline for every source","when":"Seconds matter everywhere and the team can run queues, workers and replay.","tradeoff":"More to operate, and delivery is at least once so every write must be idempotent."},{"name":"Hybrid: indexer for stable files, events and CDC for the fast sources","when":"Freshness needs differ by source, which is the usual case.","tradeoff":"Two paths must agree on the key scheme, the fingerprint and the delete rules, or the same document is written twice differently."},{"name":"Nightly batch rebuild","when":"The corpus is small and a day of staleness is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Re-embeds unchanged content, and a delete shows only after the next rebuild."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask about freshness per source, how fast deletes and revocations must land, volume and the scanned share, whether text may leave the network, who is on call and what a leak costs. Those decide the architecture, and I would not name a component before I have them."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. List the options, including the boring one.** All indexers is simplest. All events is fastest and most work. A hybrid matches freshness to each source. A nightly rebuild is the baseline that tells us what the others must beat."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick for the stated dials.** For differing freshness I would use a hybrid. A scheduled indexer with a skillset handles blob files and the SharePoint library, where minutes are fine, and I would label the SharePoint indexer as preview as of 2026-10-13 and have a push fallback. The product database and the wiki go through CDC (change feed or log-based) into a queue, and workers parse, chunk, embed and push with `mergeOrUpload`. Every chunk gets a stable key from source id and position, a parent id, and a fingerprint of text, parser, chunker and embedding model, so retries and replays are safe because delivery is at least once."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Parsing by class:** native text for clean files, layout analysis or Content Understanding for scans and tables, a local open-source parser if text must stay in the network, and a vision model only for pages whose meaning is a picture.","**Deletes:** a soft-delete policy from the first run on Blob and Cosmos DB sources, tombstones through the event path, chunk removal by parent id, and a nightly reconciliation of source ids against index keys.","**Permissions:** capture ACL ids and sensitivity on every chunk at ingest, with a re-sync path for permission changes. Knowledge source ACL ingestion is preview.","**PII:** run the PII skill before embedding where answers never need the value, and keep the value with permissions where they do.","**Versioning:** a new index per embedding model behind an alias, validated on labelled queries, with a rollback window."]},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip the choice.**"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Seconds of freshness for every source: move the indexer sources onto events too.","No platform team: prefer indexers and a Foundry IQ knowledge source, and accept the slower path.","Text may not leave the network: self-host parsing and embedding.","A deletion deadline of minutes: events plus soft-delete flags, because the indexer floor is 5 minutes before run time.","A model retirement date is near: schedule the blue-green re-embed first."]},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and detection.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Failure","How I would detect it"],"rows":[["A deleted document still answers","Reconciliation drift count, and a canary delete test per source"],["Poison file blocks the queue","Dead-letter depth and age of the oldest item"],["Retry storm on the embedding API","429 rate, queue depth, worker concurrency"],["Chunks visible to the wrong people","A restricted-user test set and ACL field coverage per chunk"],["Parser upgrade silently changes chunks","Regression set of hard pages, and the fingerprint change rate"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Freshness lag at p95 from source change to searchable, drift counts, parse success per document class, dead-letter depth and age, embedding spend per changed document and permission-sync lag. Roll out by building the new pipeline into a shadow index, comparing it with the current one on labelled queries and deletion canaries, then canary the alias."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"The line that earns marks","md":"Say how you prove the index equals the source: ids and fingerprints reconciled, not counts compared."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about freshness per source, delete and revocation deadlines, volume, residency and ownership before naming components","w":3},{"pt":"Offers real options including the boring ones, and mixes paths by source freshness","w":2},{"pt":"Makes writes idempotent with stable ids and a fingerprint, and knows delivery is at least once","w":3},{"pt":"Handles deletes per source and adds reconciliation because hard deletes are invisible to indexers","w":3},{"pt":"Routes parsing by document class and names the version and preview status of what it picks","w":2},{"pt":"Captures ACLs and PII handling at ingest, before embedding, with a re-sync path","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with a detection signal and designs dead letters, backoff and replay","w":2},{"pt":"Plans blue-green re-embedding behind an alias and a staged rollout","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"The embedding model is retired in eighteen months. What do you do today?","a":"Check the retirement schedule, then make the fingerprint include the model so the pipeline can rebuild on change. Build the new index beside the old one, validate on labelled queries, repoint the alias, and keep the old index for a rollback window."},{"q":"A user is removed from a group at 10:00 and can still retrieve a document at 10:30. Why?","a":"The permission was captured at ingest and not re-synced, so chunk ACLs are stale. I would shorten the sync for permission changes, check live for sensitive documents and measure the permission-sync lag as a metric."},{"q":"Half of the scanned PDFs have tables that parse badly. What do you do next?","a":"Score tables per document type on a labelled set, try layout analysis or Content Understanding against a local parser, keep the raw table for the model, and route only the worst pages to a vision model with validation."}],"redFlags":["Says the indexer takes care of deletes without asking how the index is reconciled with the source","Treats exactly-once delivery as a given and skips idempotent writes","Compares counts instead of ids and fingerprints to prove the index is correct","Adds permissions only at query time, with nothing captured at ingest"]},"refs":["r3","r4","r6","r7","r12","r13","r14","r33","r30"],"takeaways":["Clarify freshness per source, delete and revocation deadlines, volume, residency and ownership before choosing a pipeline","A hybrid of indexers and an event path is the usual answer, joined by one key scheme and one fingerprint","Prove correctness with reconciled ids and fingerprints, not counts, and alert on drift and dead-letter age","Capture permissions and PII decisions at ingest, and re-embed by blue-green behind an alias"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w03-t01","order":1,"group":"Architecture","title":"Ingestion architectures: indexer, push, event-driven, batch","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Four ways to get content into a search index: a managed indexer pulls on a schedule, your code pushes documents, an event pipeline reacts to changes, or a batch job rebuilds in bulk.","useIf":"You must pick how content reaches the index and can state the freshness target, the source type and who will operate the pipeline.","avoidIf":"You treat the choice as permanent: most real systems mix a pull path for bulk content with a push path for hot content.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Pull** means an indexer connects to a supported data source, detects what changed and can run a skillset (chunk, embed, OCR) on the way in [[tr1]]. You declare it and Azure AI Search runs it. The smallest schedule interval is 5 minutes and the longest is 1,440 minutes [[tr3]], and you should plan for runs that stop at about 2 hours [[tr31]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Push** means your code calls the Index Documents API with the actions `upload`, `merge`, `mergeOrUpload` and `delete`. A request carries at most 1,000 documents or 16 MB, and HTTP 207 means some documents failed while 503 means wait and retry [[tr2]]. You own parsing, chunking and embedding, which is the price of full control."},{"t":"p","md":"**Event-driven** is push with a trigger. A blob event, a Cosmos DB change feed entry or a Debezium record lands on a queue, a worker parses and embeds, then pushes. Event Grid and Debezium both deliver at least once, so the worker must be idempotent [[tr26]] [[tr25]]. **Batch** is a scheduled or one-off job that rebuilds or backfills the whole corpus, and it is the simplest way to re-embed."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t01-d1","title":"Four paths into one index","alt":"Sources feed an indexer, an event queue with a worker, or a batch job. The worker and the batch job write through the push API. The indexer and the push API both write to the search index.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[Sources] --> I[Indexer pull]\n  S --> E[Events or CDC]\n  E --> Q[Queue]\n  Q --> W[Worker]\n  W --> P[Push API]\n  S --> B[Batch job]\n  B --> P\n  I --> X[(Search index)]\n  P --> X"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Mix paths on purpose","md":"Use the indexer for the long tail of stable files and a push path for the few sources where seconds matter. Both can write to the same index if they agree on the document key (see the idempotency topic)."}],"whereToUse":["Indexer: knowledge bases over Blob Storage, ADLS Gen2, OneLake, Azure SQL or Cosmos DB where hourly or daily freshness is fine","Push: SaaS tools, wikis and internal systems that no indexer can read, or content that needs custom parsing","Event-driven: support tickets, chat transcripts or order data that must be searchable within seconds","Batch: first load, backfills and re-embedding after a model change","Foundry IQ knowledge sources, which generate an indexer pipeline for you when your source kind is supported [[tr13]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Pick the indexer when the source is a supported GA store, minutes of staleness are acceptable and built-in skills cover the work","Pick push or event-driven when you need seconds of freshness, custom parsing, dead-letter handling or a source with no indexer","Pick batch when the data changes rarely or you are rebuilding the whole index","Keep the sink idempotent whatever the path, because retries and replays happen"],"avoid":["Do not run an event pipeline for a corpus that changes nightly: you pay for queues, workers and on-call for no freshness gain","Do not use an indexer for a source whose indexer is still preview when you need a production service level","Do not hand-write a push pipeline to chunk and embed files that a skillset already handles"],"flips":["Freshness target drops from hours to seconds: move the hot path from indexer to event-driven push","The source has no indexer or the indexer is preview and you need a firm SLA: push from your own code","The team has no capacity to run queues and workers: accept minutes of staleness and use the indexer"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Scheduled indexer","Supported GA store, minutes to hours of staleness, built-in skills","Seconds of freshness, exotic sources, custom parsing","5 minute floor, per-run time caps, preview sources"],["Push from your code","Custom parsing, any source, per-document control","Small team with no ops capacity","You own retries, batching limits and monitoring"],["Event-driven push","Seconds of freshness, replay and dead-letter needs","Corpus changes rarely","At-least-once delivery, ordering gaps, queue operations"],["Batch ETL","Backfills, re-embedding, snapshots","Continuous change","Stale between runs, large bursts of embedding calls"],["Foundry IQ knowledge source","You want the pipeline generated for a supported kind","You need custom chunking or a kind still in preview","Several kinds are preview, check status first"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"New files show up much later than the schedule suggests","cause":"The schedule is only the minimum interval. A big backlog keeps one run busy until it hits the run time cap","fix":"Check run history, split the source, give the hot path to a push pipeline"},{"symptom":"Push requests fail with 413 or return 207","cause":"A batch above 1,000 documents or 16 MB, or some documents failed validation","fix":"Cap batch size, read the per-document status, retry only failed keys and back off on 503"},{"symptom":"The same chunk is indexed twice after a retry","cause":"Event delivery is at least once and the key was generated per attempt","fix":"Derive the key from the source id and chunk position, and use `mergeOrUpload`"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t01-s1","title":"Policies in blob storage, hourly is fine","situation":"A small team indexes a few thousand PDFs and Word files that live in Blob Storage. Editors change a handful per day. Nobody on the team wants to run extra infrastructure.","constraints":["Source is a supported GA store","Staleness of an hour is acceptable","Two engineers, no on-call rota","Standard layout parsing is enough"],"unknowns":["Is any document needed within minutes of publishing?","Do files need custom parsing that skills cannot do?"],"question":"How do you ingest?","options":[{"name":"Scheduled indexer with a skillset","verdict":"best","why":"It matches the source, the freshness and the team size, and built-in change detection handles updates.","tradeoff":"5 minute minimum interval and limited control over parsing."},{"name":"Event-driven push with Event Grid and a worker","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds queues, workers and dead-letter handling to buy seconds nobody asked for.","tradeoff":"Right when freshness is seconds or the parsing is custom."},{"name":"Nightly batch rebuild","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and cheap to reason about, and it fits the update rate.","tradeoff":"Re-embeds unchanged files and stays stale up to a day."}],"flips":[{"if":"Editors need changes searchable within a minute","then":"Add an event-driven push path for those folders and keep the indexer for the rest."},{"if":"Files need a proprietary parser","then":"Push from your code, since a skillset cannot run it."}],"takeaway":"Match the pipeline to the freshness target and the team, not to the most sophisticated option."},{"id":"w03-t01-s2","title":"Tickets searchable in seconds","situation":"Support tickets live in a SaaS tool that can call a webhook on every change. Agents expect a new ticket to be findable within 30 seconds. Volume is high and bursty after incidents.","constraints":["Freshness target 30 seconds","Source has no indexer","Bursty volume","A platform team can run queues"],"unknowns":["How large can a burst be?","What happens to a ticket that keeps failing?"],"question":"Which architecture fits?","options":[{"name":"Webhook to queue to worker to push API","verdict":"best","why":"The queue absorbs bursts, the worker can retry and dead-letter, and push gives seconds of freshness.","tradeoff":"More moving parts and an idempotent worker are required."},{"name":"Scheduled indexer every 5 minutes","verdict":"poor","why":"The source has no indexer and the 5 minute floor misses the 30 second target.","tradeoff":"Right for supported stores where minutes of lag are fine."},{"name":"Webhook straight to the push API","verdict":"good","why":"Fewer parts and low latency for light traffic.","tradeoff":"A burst or a throttled embedding call loses events, with no replay or dead-letter path."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume is low and a lost event is cheap to repair","then":"Direct webhook to push is acceptable."},{"if":"Freshness relaxes to 15 minutes","then":"A scheduled export plus batch push is simpler."}],"takeaway":"Seconds of freshness and bursts both argue for a queue in front of the push call."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"The business wants new documents searchable within 30 seconds. Which statement about indexers is correct?","options":["An indexer can be scheduled every 10 seconds","The smallest indexer schedule interval is 5 minutes, so a push or event path is needed","Indexers push documents faster than the Index Documents API","Indexers cannot run skillsets"],"answer":1,"explain":"The schedule floor is 5 minutes, so seconds of freshness need a push path. Indexers cannot run every 10 seconds. They do not beat push on latency. They can run skillsets, which is a reason to choose them elsewhere.","ref":"tr3"},{"id":"w03-t01-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"You must push 4,500 small documents. Each request may carry at most 1,000 documents. What is the minimum number of requests?","answer":5,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","explain":"4,500 divided by 1,000 is 4.5, so you need 5 requests. The 16 MB limit also applies, so check payload size if documents are large.","ref":"tr2"},{"id":"w03-t01-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which are real reasons to prefer your own push pipeline over an indexer?","options":["The source has no indexer","You need custom parsing the skillset cannot do","You want to avoid writing any code","You need to dead-letter poison documents and replay them"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"No indexer, custom parsing and replay control all favour push. Avoiding code is the reason to choose the indexer, so it is not a reason to leave it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the smallest and largest indexer schedule intervals?","back":"5 minutes and 1,440 minutes (24 hours), set as an XSD duration such as `PT2H`."},{"front":"Push API batch limits and status codes?","back":"1,000 documents or 16 MB per request. 413 is too large, 207 is partial failure, 503 means wait and retry."},{"front":"Why must event-driven workers be idempotent?","back":"Event Grid and Debezium deliver at least once, so the same change can arrive twice. The sink must tolerate replays."}],"related":["w03-t02","w03-t06","w03-t07","w03-t08","w04-t01"],"refs":["tr1","tr2","tr3","tr13","tr25","tr26","tr31","tr37"]},{"id":"w03-t02","order":2,"group":"Architecture","title":"Source connectors and access patterns","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A connector choice is three questions: does an indexer exist and in what release state, how does the source say something changed or was deleted, and how do you authenticate without being throttled.","useIf":"You are choosing how to read SharePoint, Blob or ADLS Gen2, OneLake, SQL, Cosmos DB or the web into an index.","avoidIf":"You pick a connector by popularity: preview status, delete visibility and throttling decide whether it survives production.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Azure AI Search indexers are GA for Blob Storage (ADLS Gen2 goes through the blob indexer), Cosmos DB for NoSQL, Azure SQL Database, SQL Managed Instance, Table Storage and OneLake. SharePoint in Microsoft 365, Azure MySQL, Cosmos DB for MongoDB and Gremlin are preview, and Cosmos DB for Cassandra is not supported [[tr1]]. That overview page was last updated 2025-06-23, so confirm status on each source page."},{"t":"p","md":"The second question is the **change and delete signal**. Blob uses the `LastModified` timestamp and does not track deletes by default [[tr4]]. Azure SQL offers integrated change tracking (tables only, sees deleted rows) or a high-water-mark column [[tr5]]. Cosmos DB uses `_ts` and needs a Boolean soft-delete field because hard deletes are invisible [[tr6]]. The SharePoint indexer detects deleted files on the next run, but it is preview and its user-delegated tokens expire every 75 minutes [[tr7]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The third question is **access pattern**. Sources throttle: Microsoft Graph and SharePoint answer with 429 or 503 and a `Retry-After` header, and ignoring it makes throttling worse [[tr29]] [[tr30]]. Reduce concurrency, spread bursts and honour the header. For sources without an indexer (SaaS tools, wikis, the open web) you push from your own code, or use a remote knowledge source that queries at request time [[tr13]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Source, indexer state and signals (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Source","Indexer","Change signal","Delete signal"],"rows":[["Blob Storage, ADLS Gen2","GA","`LastModified`","Native soft delete or a metadata flag, set up before the first run"],["Azure SQL, SQL MI","GA","Integrated change tracking or high-water mark","Tracking sees deletes, or a soft-delete column"],["Cosmos DB for NoSQL","GA","`_ts` high-water mark","Boolean soft-delete field only"],["OneLake","GA","Check the OneLake indexer page","Check the OneLake indexer page"],["SharePoint in Microsoft 365","Preview","Per run crawl","Detected on the next run"],["SaaS, wiki, web","None","Your code","Your code"]]}],"whereToUse":["Choosing the first connector for a knowledge base and checking its release state before committing","Estimating delete behaviour before promising the business that removed content disappears from answers","Planning concurrency and retry settings for large first crawls of SharePoint or SaaS APIs","Deciding between an indexed knowledge source and a remote one in Foundry IQ","Reviewing a design where a source has no indexer and the team must write a reader"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the GA indexer for Blob, SQL, Cosmos DB and OneLake when staleness of minutes is fine and the delete signal exists","Use a custom reader with push when the source has no indexer or the indexer is preview and you need a firm service level","Use a remote knowledge source when the content must be live and you do not want to copy it","Use change tracking over high-water marks on SQL tables, since it also sees deletes"],"avoid":["Avoid preview indexers for production workloads that have a no-preview policy","Avoid assuming deletes propagate: Blob and Cosmos DB indexers only see soft deletes configured from the first run","Avoid unbounded parallel crawls of Graph or SharePoint, which trigger 429 and 503 responses"],"flips":["Policy forbids preview features: sync SharePoint content with your own reader and push, carrying ACL fields yourself","Source is a view rather than a table: switch from integrated change tracking to a high-water mark plus a soft-delete column","Freshness must be live and copying is not allowed: choose a remote knowledge source and accept query-time latency"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["GA indexer (Blob, SQL, Cosmos DB, OneLake)","Supported store, change signal exists","Source rows are hard-deleted or the structure needs custom parsing","Delete blind spots, per-run time caps"],["Preview indexer (SharePoint)","Prototype or acceptable preview risk, least code","No-preview policy or a firm SLA","API changes, token expiry every 75 minutes"],["Custom reader plus push","No indexer, custom auth or parsing","Small team, no on-call","You own throttling, retries and ACL capture"],["Remote knowledge source (Foundry IQ)","Live content, no copy allowed","You need ranking control or offline access","Latency per query, several kinds are preview"],["Export to Blob then blob indexer","A GA path from a source with only export","Permissions and fast change matter","Extra hop, ACLs lost unless carried as metadata"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Deleted records or files still appear in answers","cause":"The Blob or Cosmos DB indexer cannot see hard deletes and no soft-delete policy existed on the first run","fix":"Add a soft-delete flag and policy, or run a reconciliation job that compares source ids with index keys"},{"symptom":"A big SharePoint crawl stops part way or the crawler gets blocked","cause":"User-delegated tokens expire every 75 minutes, or immediate retries after 429 count against the usage limits","fix":"Plan the first load in resumable slices, honour `Retry-After`, cap concurrency and check the current auth guidance"},{"symptom":"Moved folders are not re-indexed","cause":"Renaming an ADLS Gen2 directory does not update blob timestamps","fix":"Touch the files or reset the indexer after bulk moves"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t02-s1","title":"SharePoint sites with a no-preview policy","situation":"An assistant must answer from about 20,000 SharePoint pages and files. Access differs by site. Security policy bans preview features in production and go-live is next month.","constraints":["Source is SharePoint","No preview features in production","Permissions differ by site","Small team"],"unknowns":["Is any document needed within minutes of editing?","Who owns ACL sync after launch?"],"question":"How do you connect to SharePoint?","options":[{"name":"Own reader using Microsoft Graph, pushing chunks with ACL fields","verdict":"best","why":"It stays on GA surfaces, lets you capture site permissions on every chunk and gives you control over throttling.","tradeoff":"You build and run the reader and the permission refresh."},{"name":"Copy files to Blob Storage and use the blob indexer","verdict":"good","why":"Both pieces are GA and cheap to run.","tradeoff":"Permissions and deletes are lost unless the copy job carries them as metadata."},{"name":"SharePoint indexer in preview","verdict":"poor","why":"It is the least code but it breaks the no-preview rule.","tradeoff":"Right when preview risk is acceptable and speed to prototype matters."}],"flips":[{"if":"Policy allows preview and the team is tiny","then":"The SharePoint indexer wins on effort."},{"if":"Content must stay in SharePoint and never be copied","then":"A remote knowledge source, if its preview state is acceptable, avoids the copy."}],"takeaway":"Release state is a requirement, not a footnote: it can rule out the easiest connector."},{"id":"w03-t02-s2","title":"Orders table with frequent deletes","situation":"A relational table of about five million rows changes every minute. Cancelled and purged rows must leave search results the same day. Freshness of 10 minutes is fine.","constraints":["Azure SQL table, not a view","Deletes must propagate within a day","10 minute freshness","Team can run one scheduled job"],"unknowns":["Are rows deleted physically or flagged?","Is change tracking enabled on the database?"],"question":"Which pattern do you pick?","options":[{"name":"SQL indexer with integrated change tracking","verdict":"best","why":"It is recommended for tables and detects deleted rows without a flag.","tradeoff":"Change tracking must be enabled on the source and it does not work on views."},{"name":"SQL indexer with a high-water-mark column","verdict":"good","why":"Works for tables and views and needs only a rowversion or last-updated column.","tradeoff":"Needs a soft-delete column too, or purged rows stay in the index."},{"name":"Nightly full reload into a new index","verdict":"poor","why":"It re-embeds unchanged rows every night and leaves answers stale for up to a day.","tradeoff":"Right for a small table or when no change signal exists."}],"flips":[{"if":"The source is a view","then":"Use the high-water mark with a soft-delete column."},{"if":"Deletes arrive as separate purge jobs outside the table","then":"Add a reconciliation job comparing ids."}],"takeaway":"Pick the change signal that also reveals deletes."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A removed Cosmos DB document still shows in search. Which cause is most likely?","options":["The document was hard-deleted and no soft-delete field exists","The semantic ranker cached it","The `_ts` field is not indexed","The embedding model changed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Cosmos DB indexer detects deletes only through a pre-existing soft-delete field, so a hard delete is invisible. Ranker caching, `_ts` indexing and model changes do not explain a document that is still present.","ref":"tr6"},{"id":"w03-t02-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each source with its change signal for indexers.","pairs":[{"l":"Blob Storage","r":"`LastModified` timestamp"},{"l":"Azure SQL table","r":"Integrated change tracking"},{"l":"Cosmos DB for NoSQL","r":"`_ts` high-water mark"},{"l":"SharePoint indexer","r":"Crawl on each run, preview"}],"explain":"Each source exposes a different signal and only some also reveal deletes. SharePoint is a preview indexer that re-detects deleted files on the next run.","ref":"tr1"},{"id":"w03-t02-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Your crawler gets 429 from SharePoint and retries immediately. What happens?","options":["Retries succeed faster","Nothing, 429 is informational","Throttled calls count against usage limits, so it gets worse and can be blocked","The server resets its limits"],"answer":2,"explain":"Throttled requests count towards usage limits, so you must wait for `Retry-After` and reduce concurrency. 429 is an error, not a hint, and limits do not reset on request.","ref":"tr30"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which Azure AI Search indexer data sources are GA and which are preview?","back":"GA: Blob (incl. ADLS Gen2), Cosmos DB NoSQL, Azure SQL, SQL MI, Table, OneLake. Preview: SharePoint, MySQL, Cosmos Mongo and Gremlin. Cassandra unsupported."},{"front":"How do Blob and Cosmos DB indexers learn about deletes?","back":"Only through a soft-delete signal set up before the first run: blob soft delete or a metadata flag, and a Boolean field in Cosmos DB."},{"front":"What should a crawler do on 429 or 503 from Graph or SharePoint?","back":"Wait the `Retry-After` seconds, reduce concurrency and spread bursts. Immediate retries count against limits."}],"related":["w03-t01","w03-t06","w03-t09","w03-t11","w07-t12"],"refs":["tr1","tr4","tr5","tr6","tr7","tr13","tr29","tr30"]},{"id":"w03-t03","order":3,"group":"Parsing","title":"Text extraction, OCR and layout analysis","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Three ways to turn a file into text: read the embedded text layer, run OCR on page images, or run layout analysis that also recovers reading order, headings, tables and figures.","useIf":"Your files include scans, multi-column pages, tables or forms and you must decide how much parsing each document type deserves.","avoidIf":"Every file is clean born-digital text: a heavy parser adds cost and a new failure surface for no gain.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Native extraction** reads the text layer already inside a born-digital PDF or Office file. It is fast and cheap, but it knows nothing about structure: columns can interleave and a table becomes a stream of cells. **OCR** reads pixels from scans and photos and returns text, still without structure. **Layout analysis** adds the structure: reading order, headings, tables, figures and selection marks."},{"t":"p","md":"Azure Document Intelligence layout v4.0 (`2024-11-30`, GA) returns Markdown when you set `outputContentFormat=markdown`, renders tables as HTML tables with merged cells and multi-row headers, shows selection marks as ☒ and ☐, flags handwriting per line in `styles` with a confidence, and describes figures with `boundingRegions`, `spans` and `elements`. For Word, Excel, PowerPoint and HTML it extracts embedded text only, not embedded images [[tr16]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Inside Azure AI Search, the Document Layout skill wraps that model. `outputFormat` is `markdown` (default) or `text`, `markdownHeaderDepth` runs from `h1` to `h6` (default `h6`) and `outputMode` is `oneToMany`. Its `chunkingProperties` apply only to `text` output. It times out when a document needs over 5 minutes, and a billable Foundry resource is required beyond 20 documents per indexer per day [[tr17]]. Service limits per request are 500 MB and 2,000 pages on S0 [[tr34]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Open-source parsers such as Docling (MIT-licensed code, runs locally, handles layout, reading order, tables and OCR for scans) cover the same ground when data cannot leave your network [[tr20]]. Quality varies by document type, which is why benchmarks such as OmniDocBench exist [[tr22]]. Treat the parser as a versioned dependency and test it on your own pages."},{"t":"flow","title":"Route each document to the cheapest parser that keeps its structure","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Classify","md":"Born-digital with a text layer, scan, or mixed. Count pages and tables.","tag":"cheap"},{"title":"Extract natively","md":"Use the text layer for clean single-column files.","tag":"text"},{"title":"Analyse layout","md":"Scans, columns, tables, forms and figures go to layout analysis with Markdown output.","tag":"structure"},{"title":"Check a sample","md":"Compare against a labelled set per document type and pin the parser version.","tag":"test"}]}],"whereToUse":["Contracts, manuals and reports that mix prose, tables and figures and feed structure-aware chunking","Scanned archives where text is only available through OCR","Forms and invoices with selection marks and handwriting","Air-gapped or regulated environments that parse locally with an open-source tool","Pre-processing step before a Text Split or semantic chunking stage"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use native extraction for clean born-digital single-column text","Use layout analysis when tables, columns, forms or figures carry meaning","Use OCR-only output when scans are plain text and you do not need structure","Use a local parser when data residency or an air gap forbids a cloud service"],"avoid":["Avoid layout analysis on every file by default: it costs more per page and adds time","Avoid native extraction for multi-column or table-heavy PDFs without checking reading order","Avoid unpinned parser versions: a silent upgrade changes chunk text and your retrieval metrics"],"flips":["Pages are mostly charts and slides: consider a vision model or page-image embeddings instead of text parsing","Volume is very high and data must stay local: open-source parsing on your own compute beats a per-page service","You need page coordinates for citations: pick a parser that returns regions such as `boundingRegions`"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Native text extraction","Clean born-digital text","Scans, columns, tables","Cheapest, but reading order and tables can be wrong"],["OCR only","Plain scanned text","You need tables, headings or forms","Text without structure, handwriting errors"],["Layout analysis (Document Intelligence)","Tables, forms, figures, citations with regions","Very high volume on a tight budget","Per-page charges, 5 minute skill timeout"],["Open-source local parser (Docling, Unstructured)","Air gap, high volume, full control","You cannot run or tune models","Compute and upgrade churn, pin versions"],["Vision model per page","Charts and visual layouts","You need deterministic output","Cost and variability, see the vision topic"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Parsing code that read `:selected:` tokens suddenly finds no checked boxes","cause":"Layout v4.0 returns ☒ and ☐ and tables as HTML, not the older tokens","fix":"Update the parser code, keep a regression set of forms"},{"symptom":"Large PDFs fail or are billed without output","cause":"The Document Layout skill times out after 5 minutes of layout work and is still charged when a Foundry resource is attached","fix":"Split very long documents and filter by page count before the skill"},{"symptom":"Answers mix two unrelated sentences","cause":"Native extraction read a two-column page across the columns","fix":"Use layout analysis for multi-column files and spot-check reading order"},{"symptom":"Retrieval quality drops after a dependency bump","cause":"The parser changed output (Docling v2.138.0 shipped 2026-10-12, Unstructured 0.27.25 on 2026-10-10) [[tr21]]","fix":"Pin versions and run a parsing regression set before upgrading"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t03-s1","title":"Mixed archive on a budget","situation":"A corpus of 300,000 files is mostly born-digital PDFs, with a smaller share of scans and some table-heavy reports. The budget is tight and answers must quote tables correctly.","constraints":["Cost per page matters at this volume","Tables must survive","Cloud processing is allowed","A labelled test set of 200 pages exists"],"unknowns":["What share of pages are scans or tables?","Is page-level citation required?"],"question":"How do you parse?","options":[{"name":"Route by type: native text for clean PDFs, layout analysis for scans and table-heavy files","verdict":"best","why":"You spend per-page money only where structure matters and verify on the labelled set.","tradeoff":"You build a classifier and maintain two paths."},{"name":"Layout analysis for every file","verdict":"good","why":"One path with consistent Markdown output and tables everywhere.","tradeoff":"Pays for structure on files that have none."},{"name":"OCR-only text for every file","verdict":"poor","why":"It flattens tables and loses reading order, which breaks table questions.","tradeoff":"Right for plain scanned prose with no tables."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most files turn out to contain tables","then":"Layout analysis everywhere is simpler and not much dearer."},{"if":"Budget is unlimited and uniform output is valued","then":"A single layout path wins on simplicity."}],"takeaway":"Route by document type and measure on a labelled sample."},{"id":"w03-t03-s2","title":"Sensitive documents that cannot leave the network","situation":"A team must index internal reports that policy says cannot be sent to external cloud services. Volume is moderate and layout quality still matters.","constraints":["No external cloud processing","Layout and tables needed","Engineers can run services on their own hosts","Updates are monthly"],"unknowns":["What licences apply to the models the parser downloads?","Is a GPU available?"],"question":"Which parser approach fits?","options":[{"name":"Local open-source parser such as Docling, pinned and regression tested","verdict":"best","why":"It runs locally, covers layout, tables and OCR, and the code is MIT licensed.","tradeoff":"You own compute, upgrades and quality tuning, and model licences vary."},{"name":"Native text extraction only","verdict":"good","why":"Local and cheap, and fine for born-digital text.","tradeoff":"Tables and columns may be wrong, so answers about them degrade."},{"name":"Cloud layout service with sample data","verdict":"poor","why":"It breaks the no-external-processing rule.","tradeoff":"Right when residency and policy allow the service."}],"flips":[{"if":"A compliant private deployment of a managed service is approved","then":"The managed layout service reduces operations."},{"if":"Files are clean text only","then":"Native extraction is enough."}],"takeaway":"Residency can decide the parser before quality does."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After upgrading to layout v4.0 `2024-11-30` your checkbox parser returns nothing. What changed?","options":["Selection marks are now Unicode ☒ and ☐ and tables are HTML","Checkboxes are returned only as images","Selection marks were removed","The output switched to JSON only"],"answer":0,"explain":"v4.0 renders selection marks as ☒ and ☐ instead of `:selected:` and `:unselected:` tokens, and Markdown tables become HTML tables. The others are not documented behaviour.","ref":"tr16"},{"id":"w03-t03-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 5,300 page document must be split to stay under the 2,000 page S0 request limit. What is the minimum number of requests?","answer":3,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","explain":"5,300 divided by 2,000 is 2.65, so you need 3 requests. File size (500 MB) and the skill's 5 minute timeout also apply.","ref":"tr34"},{"id":"w03-t03-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which signals argue for layout analysis over native extraction?","options":["Two-column pages","Tables that carry the answer","Plain single-column born-digital text","Forms with checkboxes"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Columns, tables and forms depend on structure that a text layer lacks. Plain born-digital text is the case where native extraction is enough."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you get Markdown from Document Intelligence layout?","back":"Set `outputContentFormat=markdown` (v4.0 `2024-11-30`, GA). Tables come as HTML tables and selection marks as ☒ and ☐."},{"front":"Document Layout skill: output formats and the timeout?","back":"`outputFormat` is `markdown` (default) or `text`. Documents needing over 5 minutes in the layout model time out."},{"front":"When does a local parser such as Docling beat a cloud layout service?","back":"When data cannot leave your network or volume makes per-page pricing hard. You then own compute, upgrades and tuning."}],"related":["w03-t04","w03-t05","w04-t05","w04-t11"],"refs":["tr16","tr17","tr20","tr21","tr22","tr34","tr36","tr38"]},{"id":"w03-t04","order":4,"group":"Parsing","title":"Vision LLMs and Content Understanding for parsing","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A vision model reads a page image directly and can explain charts and messy layouts that OCR flattens. Azure Content Understanding wraps analysis in schema-defined fields with confidence and grounding.","useIf":"Pages carry meaning in charts, diagrams or irregular layouts, or you need typed fields with confidence and grounding rather than free text.","avoidIf":"Documents are clean text or ordinary tables and you need cheap, repeatable output: layout analysis wins on cost and determinism.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **vision LLM** takes a page image and a prompt and returns text, Markdown or JSON. It can describe a chart, read a stamp or follow an odd layout that OCR turns into noise. The costs are real: image and output tokens per page, latency, and output that can differ between runs. Those costs and the determinism point below are engineering practice, not vendor figures: measure them on your pages."},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure Content Understanding** analyses documents, images, audio and video into schema-defined fields with confidence scores and grounding. API `2025-11-01` is GA and recommended for production. `2026-06-01-preview` adds signature detection, document metadata extraction and richer classification with in-page segmentation [[tr18]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Inside Azure AI Search, the Content Understanding skill is GA in REST `2026-04-01`. It extracts and chunks in one skill, can let chunks span pages, and its `chunkingProperties` set `method` (`fixedSize` by default, `semantic` in preview since `2026-05-01-preview`), `unit` (`characters`, or `tokens` with `semantic` only), `maximumLength` (300 to 50,000 characters or 100 to 8,000 tokens, default 500) and `overlapLength` (below half of `maximumLength`, `fixedSize` only). AI image descriptions are preview and need an Azure OpenAI chat model on the attached Foundry resource [[tr19]]. Asynchronous documents are limited to 200 MB and 300 pages [[tr35]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Determinism matters for ingestion.** If a vision parse changes a few words on every run, your content hash changes, unchanged files look new and you re-embed them. Cache the parse keyed by file hash, parser version and prompt version, and re-parse only on purpose. Benchmarks such as OmniDocBench compare pipeline tools with end-to-end vision-language models, and ColPali skips parsing by embedding page images as multi-vector embeddings [[tr22]] [[tr39]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Validate vision parsing before you trust it","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Parse","md":"Send the page image with a fixed prompt and a JSON schema.","tag":"vision"},{"title":"Schema check","md":"Reject output that fails types or required fields.","tag":"auto"},{"title":"Cross-check","md":"Confirm every number appears in the OCR or layout text of the same page.","tag":"auto"},{"title":"Sample","md":"A person reviews a slice per document type.","tag":"human"},{"title":"Cache","md":"Store output with file hash, parser and prompt version.","tag":"store"}]}],"whereToUse":["Slide decks, dashboards and infographics where charts carry the facts","Invoices, forms and statements where typed fields with confidence feed a downstream system","Handwritten or irregular pages where classic OCR output is unusable","Figure captioning at ingest so charts become searchable text","Mixed media (documents, audio, video) analysed under one schema with Content Understanding"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use a vision model when the answer lives in a chart, diagram or irregular layout","Use Content Understanding when you need schema-defined fields, confidence scores and grounding","Use the Content Understanding skill when you want extraction and chunking in one indexing step","Use caching and validation for every model-based parser"],"avoid":["Avoid a vision model as the default for plain text files: cost and variability buy nothing","Avoid trusting transcribed numbers without a cross-check against a second source","Avoid semantic chunking and image descriptions in production until their preview status changes"],"flips":["Pages are mostly visual and retrieval of whole pages is enough: embed page images instead of parsing","Output must be reproducible for audit: prefer a layout model or cache every parse with versions","Volume is huge and layouts are uniform: a template-based layout model is cheaper than a vision call per page"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["OCR or layout model","Text, tables, forms, uniform volume","Charts and diagrams carry the meaning","Misses visual content"],["Content Understanding analyzer","Typed fields with confidence and grounding","You need free-form page description","Schema design effort, preview features in `2026-06-01-preview`"],["General vision LLM with a prompt","Charts, odd layouts, quick prototypes","Reproducibility or low cost per page","Variable output, hallucinated numbers, token cost"],["Page-image embeddings (ColPali style)","Visually rich pages, no parsing wanted","You need text for keyword search or citations","Multi-vector index size and a different retrieval stack"],["Hybrid: layout text plus vision captions for figures","Mixed pages","Budget cannot carry two passes","Two pipelines to keep in sync"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Unchanged files are re-embedded on every run","cause":"A vision parse returns slightly different text each time, so the content hash changes","fix":"Cache parse output by file hash plus parser and prompt version, and hash the cached text"},{"symptom":"A table total is wrong in the answer","cause":"The vision model transcribed a number incorrectly and nothing checked it","fix":"Cross-check numbers against layout text and route mismatches to review"},{"symptom":"Pipeline breaks when semantic chunking is enabled","cause":"Semantic chunking is preview in `2026-05-01-preview` and the GA API does not offer it","fix":"Use `fixedSize` on `2026-04-01` and evaluate semantic chunking separately"},{"symptom":"Tables arrive as plain text from one skill and as Markdown from another","cause":"The Content Understanding skill page and the Document Layout skill page disagree on table output","fix":"Test both skills on your own tables before choosing"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t04-s1","title":"Quarterly decks full of charts","situation":"An assistant answers questions about slide decks. Many answers sit in charts and diagrams. About 2,000 decks arrive per quarter and answers need not be byte-identical between runs.","constraints":["Moderate volume","Charts carry facts","Cloud processing allowed","Budget for a second pass on figures"],"unknowns":["Do users ask for exact chart values or trends?","Can a human sample the output?"],"question":"How do you parse the decks?","options":[{"name":"Layout text for the slides, plus vision-generated captions for figures, cached and cross-checked","verdict":"best","why":"Text stays cheap and repeatable while charts become searchable sentences.","tradeoff":"Two passes and a cache to maintain."},{"name":"Vision model on every page","verdict":"good","why":"One uniform path that sees everything.","tradeoff":"Highest token cost and the most run-to-run variation."},{"name":"OCR text only","verdict":"poor","why":"Chart content never becomes text, so those questions fail.","tradeoff":"Right for text-only slides."}],"flips":[{"if":"Users need exact chart values","then":"Extract the underlying data when available and validate captions against it."},{"if":"Most decks are text only","then":"Layout analysis alone is enough."}],"takeaway":"Spend vision tokens only on the regions where text parsing cannot see."},{"id":"w03-t04-s2","title":"Invoices feeding a finance system","situation":"Scanned supplier invoices must yield supplier, total and due date for a downstream system. Auditors want to see how confident each extraction is and where on the page it came from.","constraints":["Typed fields required","Audit needs confidence and provenance","Several layouts","Production GA preferred"],"unknowns":["How many layouts and languages?","What error rate is acceptable before human review?"],"question":"What do you use?","options":[{"name":"Content Understanding analyzer with a field schema, GA API `2025-11-01`","verdict":"best","why":"It returns schema-defined fields with confidence scores and grounding, which matches the audit need.","tradeoff":"You design and test the schema and pay per analysis."},{"name":"Vision LLM with a JSON schema and your own validation","verdict":"good","why":"Flexible and quick to start.","tradeoff":"You must build confidence, grounding and caching yourself."},{"name":"OCR text plus regular expressions","verdict":"poor","why":"Several layouts break brittle patterns.","tradeoff":"Right for a single fixed template."}],"flips":[{"if":"A rare field needs the preview `2026-06-01-preview` features","then":"Isolate that path and label it preview."},{"if":"One uniform template covers 99 percent of invoices","then":"A cheaper template-based layout model suffices."}],"takeaway":"Typed fields with provenance favour an analyzer over a free-form prompt."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t04-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which Content Understanding API version is GA and recommended for production as of 2026-10-13?","options":["`2026-06-01-preview`","`2025-11-01`","`2024-11-30`","`2026-08-01-preview`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`2025-11-01` is the GA version. `2026-06-01-preview` is a preview that adds signature detection and metadata extraction. `2024-11-30` is Document Intelligence v4.0 and `2026-08-01-preview` is an AI Search preview.","ref":"tr18"},{"id":"w03-t04-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are genuine drawbacks of a general vision LLM as your page parser?","options":["Output can vary between runs","Image and output tokens add cost per page","It cannot read charts","Numbers can be mis-transcribed"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Variation, per-page token cost and transcription errors are the trade-offs. Reading charts is the strength, not a drawback."},{"id":"w03-t04-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the validation steps for vision parsing in order.","items":["Parse the page with a fixed prompt and schema","Reject output that fails the schema","Cross-check numbers against layout text","Sample for human review","Cache with file hash and versions"],"explain":"Cheap automatic checks come before human review, and the cache stores only output that survived them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why can vision-based parsing cause re-embedding churn?","back":"Small run-to-run text differences change the content hash. Cache parses by file hash, parser version and prompt version."},{"front":"Content Understanding skill chunking options?","back":"`method`: `fixedSize` (default) or `semantic` (preview). `maximumLength` default 500, `overlapLength` below half, `fixedSize` only."},{"front":"What does Content Understanding add over a plain prompt?","back":"Schema-defined fields with confidence scores and grounding across documents, images, audio and video."}],"related":["w03-t03","w03-t05","w03-t08","w04-t11"],"refs":["tr18","tr19","tr22","tr35","tr39"]},{"id":"w03-t05","order":5,"group":"Parsing","title":"Tables, figures and forms","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Tables, figures and forms hold facts that plain text parsing scrambles. You choose a representation at ingest: a table block, row sentences, a structured store, a figure caption or typed form fields.","useIf":"Answers depend on cells, charts or checkboxes and you must decide how each is stored and retrieved.","avoidIf":"Tables are tiny decoration: indexing them as ordinary text is fine.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A table is a grid whose meaning comes from its headers. Layout v4.0 returns Markdown with tables as **HTML tables** that keep merged cells and multi-row headers, and selection marks as ☒ and ☐ [[tr16]]. The Content Understanding skill outputs tables and figures as Markdown and lets chunks span pages, but its page and the Document Layout skill page disagree about the output format, so test both on your own tables [[tr19]] [[tr17]]."},{"t":"p","md":"You then pick a **representation**. Keep a small table whole so its headers travel with its values. For a large table, split by rows and repeat the header and caption in every chunk, or serialise each row as a sentence. For questions that need sums, ranks or filters, parse the table into a structured store, because an embedding of text cannot add numbers. Index projections can write one chunk per row with a parent id that links back to the source table [[tr11]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","title":"One table, row-wise representation","code":"Table: Q3 revenue by region (report.pdf, page 12)\nRegion: EMEA | Q3 revenue: 4.2M | Change: +6%\nRegion: APAC | Q3 revenue: 3.1M | Change: -2%","caption":"Each chunk repeats the caption and column names, so any single row is understandable alone."},{"t":"p","md":"**Figures** need text before they can be retrieved. Layout analysis returns each figure's `boundingRegions`, `spans` and `elements`, so you can crop it, caption it with a vision model and store the caption with the region for citations. Note that for Word, Excel, PowerPoint and HTML the layout model extracts embedded text only, not embedded images [[tr16]]. **Forms** are best stored as typed key-value fields, with selection marks as booleans."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t05-d1","title":"Where each piece of layout output goes","alt":"Layout output splits into tables and figures. Small tables stay whole, large tables become row chunks with a header, numeric questions use a structured store. Figures get a caption and a region id.","src":"flowchart TD\n  L[Layout output] --> T{Table size}\n  T -->|small| K[Keep whole table chunk]\n  T -->|large| R[Row chunks with header]\n  T -->|numeric questions| S[Structured store]\n  L --> F[Figure region]\n  F --> C[Caption plus region id]"}],"whereToUse":["Financial statements, price lists and compatibility matrices where each cell answers a question","Scientific and technical reports with figures that carry results","Forms, applications and checklists with selection marks and handwriting","Slide decks and dashboards whose charts need captions to become searchable","Any corpus where users ask for totals, comparisons or the largest value in a table"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use whole-table chunks for small tables with a few rows","Use row chunks with repeated headers and captions for large lookup tables","Use a structured store plus a query tool when questions aggregate or compare numbers","Use stored figure regions so answers can cite the page area"],"avoid":["Avoid fixed-size splitting of table text: it cuts rows and strips headers","Avoid indexing only an LLM summary of a table when users ask for exact cell values","Avoid assuming embedded images in Office files were captured by layout analysis"],"flips":["Questions become numeric aggregations: move from text chunks to a structured store with SQL or code execution","Tables span many pages with repeated headers: stitch them before chunking","Figures are decorative: skip captioning and save the vision cost"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Representation","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Whole table as HTML or Markdown chunk","Small tables, mixed text and cells","Table exceeds the chunk size","Large chunks dilute the embedding"],["Row chunks with repeated header","Lookup tables, one fact per row","Questions need many rows together","More chunks, header tokens repeated"],["LLM summary of the table","Questions about what the table shows","Users need exact values","Summaries drop cells, add a model call"],["Structured store plus query tool","Sums, ranks, filters, joins","Tables are irregular or one-off","Schema work, an extra tool and its guardrails"],["Vision caption for a figure","Charts and diagrams","Exact plotted values matter","Model cost, variable text, needs validation"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The answer names the wrong column for a value","cause":"A split table lost its header row in later chunks","fix":"Repeat the header and caption in every chunk or serialise rows as sentences"},{"symptom":"Values shift columns after parsing","cause":"Merged cells were flattened into pipe-style Markdown","fix":"Keep HTML tables from layout v4.0 and test merged-cell documents"},{"symptom":"Charts in Word and PowerPoint never appear in answers","cause":"The layout model extracts embedded text only for Office and HTML files, not embedded images","fix":"Extract embedded images separately and caption them"},{"symptom":"The model adds up a table incorrectly","cause":"Arithmetic over retrieved text cells is unreliable","fix":"Route numeric questions to a structured store or a code tool"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t05-s1","title":"Financial statements with aggregate questions","situation":"Analysts ask for totals, year-on-year change and the highest cost line across many statements. Every number must be traceable to a page.","constraints":["Numeric aggregation is common","Citations required","Statements share a few formats","Errors are costly"],"unknowns":["How regular are the statement formats?","Is a query tool allowed in the pipeline?"],"question":"How do you represent the tables?","options":[{"name":"Parse tables into a structured store with page references, and keep a text chunk per table for context","verdict":"best","why":"Sums and comparisons run as queries, and the page reference supports citations.","tradeoff":"Schema mapping work and an extra tool to secure."},{"name":"Row chunks with repeated headers","verdict":"good","why":"Good for single lookups and simple to build.","tradeoff":"Cross-row arithmetic still falls on the model."},{"name":"Fixed-size text chunks of flattened table text","verdict":"poor","why":"Cuts rows, strips headers and invites wrong cells.","tradeoff":"Fine only for tiny, headerless tables."}],"flips":[{"if":"Questions are single-cell lookups only","then":"Row chunks are enough and cheaper."},{"if":"Formats are wildly irregular","then":"Fall back to row chunks plus review for the irregular files."}],"takeaway":"Aggregation questions need a query tool, not a better embedding."},{"id":"w03-t05-s2","title":"Compatibility matrices in product manuals","situation":"Support staff ask whether product A works with accessory B. The answer sits in large matrices spread over several pages. Latency matters and exact cells are needed.","constraints":["Single-cell lookups","Tables span pages","Interactive latency","No structured store available"],"unknowns":["How many columns per matrix?","Do matrices repeat headers on each page?"],"question":"Which representation fits?","options":[{"name":"Stitch page fragments, then row chunks with caption and column names repeated","verdict":"best","why":"Each chunk answers one lookup and carries its own headers.","tradeoff":"More chunks and a stitching step."},{"name":"One whole-table chunk per matrix","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and keeps headers with values.","tradeoff":"Large chunks dilute retrieval and may exceed chunk limits."},{"name":"LLM summary of each matrix","verdict":"poor","why":"Summaries drop the exact cells the users need.","tradeoff":"Right for questions such as what a table covers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Matrices are small","then":"Whole-table chunks win on simplicity."},{"if":"A structured store becomes available","then":"Query it for exact lookups and keep chunks for context."}],"takeaway":"Make each retrievable unit understandable on its own."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Charts embedded in PowerPoint files never show up in your index. Layout analysis ran without errors. Why?","options":["Charts are always blocked by the service","The model needs a GPU","Selection marks hide them","For Word, Excel, PowerPoint and HTML the layout model extracts embedded text only, not embedded images"],"answer":3,"explain":"The documented behaviour is text only for those formats, so embedded images need separate extraction and captioning. The other options are not documented limits.","ref":"tr16"},{"id":"w03-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which choices protect the meaning of a large table split into chunks?","options":["Repeat the header row in each chunk","Include the table caption","Split every 500 characters regardless of rows","Serialise rows as `Column: value` sentences"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Repeated headers, captions and labelled row sentences keep each chunk self-explanatory. Character-based splitting cuts rows and loses headers."},{"id":"w03-t05-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each question type with the best representation.","pairs":[{"l":"Exact cell lookup in a long matrix","r":"Row chunks with repeated headers"},{"l":"Total or rank across many rows","r":"Structured store plus query tool"},{"l":"What does this chart show?","r":"Vision caption stored with region id"},{"l":"Which box is checked on a form?","r":"Typed key-value field from a selection mark"}],"explain":"Match the retrieval unit to the question. Aggregations need computation, lookups need self-contained rows, charts need text captions and forms need typed fields.","ref":"tr16"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does layout v4.0 represent tables and checkboxes?","back":"Tables as HTML tables with merged cells and multi-row headers. Selection marks as the characters ☒ and ☐."},{"front":"Why repeat the header in every table chunk?","back":"A chunk without headers has values with no column meaning, so retrieval and the model can pair values with the wrong labels."},{"front":"When should a table go to a structured store?","back":"When questions need sums, ranks, filters or joins. Embeddings of cell text cannot compute them reliably."}],"related":["w03-t03","w03-t04","w03-t11","w04-t11","w05-t11"],"refs":["tr11","tr16","tr17","tr19"]},{"id":"w03-t06","order":6,"group":"Keeping it fresh","title":"Incremental ingestion and change detection","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Incremental ingestion processes only what changed since the last run, using a timestamp, a change log or a content hash, and falls back to a full rebuild when the pipeline itself changes.","useIf":"The corpus is large, changes slowly relative to its size and you want cost and run time to follow the changes, not the corpus.","avoidIf":"The corpus is small or the source gives no reliable change signal: a scheduled full reload is simpler and safer.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A change signal answers one question: what is different since my last run? **Timestamps** are the cheapest. The Blob indexer uses `LastModified` [[tr4]], and the Cosmos DB indexer uses a high-water mark on `_ts` [[tr6]]. Azure SQL offers **integrated change tracking** (tables only, detects deleted rows, recommended) or a **high-water mark** on a `rowversion` or last-updated column, which also works for views [[tr5]]. A **content hash** compares what you stored with what you have read now, so an item that was touched but not edited costs no embedding call."},{"t":"p","md":"Signals have blind spots. Deletes are not tracked by default for Blob, and both Blob and Cosmos DB need a soft-delete policy from the first run [[tr4]] [[tr6]]. A restored blob is not re-indexed unless its `LastModified` changes, and renaming an ADLS Gen2 directory does not update blob timestamps [[tr4]]. The fix for any source with blind spots is a periodic **reconciliation**: list source ids, list index keys, and repair the difference."},{"t":"p","md":"Incremental runs are not enough when the pipeline changes. A new parser, chunker or embedding model changes every chunk, so you need a full rebuild into a new index (see the blue-green topic). For targeted replays, Reset Indexer is the GA full reset, while Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync are preview APIs in `2026-08-01-preview` [[tr9]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"One incremental run","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Find changes","md":"Ask the source for items newer than the checkpoint, or read the change log.","tag":"signal"},{"title":"Hash gate","md":"Skip items whose normalised content hash is unchanged.","tag":"cheap"},{"title":"Process","md":"Parse, chunk and embed only the changed items.","tag":"cost"},{"title":"Upsert","md":"Write with a stable key so a replay is safe.","tag":"safe"},{"title":"Reconcile and advance","md":"Repair missing or extra ids, then move the checkpoint.","tag":"truth"}]}],"whereToUse":["Large document stores where a small percentage changes each day","Database-backed catalogues with a rowversion, last-updated column or change tracking","Content that is touched by tools (migrations, virus scans) without real edits, where a hash gate saves embedding cost","Nightly reconciliation jobs that compare source ids with index keys","Pipelines that run both incremental updates and rare planned full rebuilds"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use built-in indexer change detection when the source is a supported GA store","Use SQL integrated change tracking on tables when deletes matter","Use a content hash when timestamps are noisy or edits are cosmetic","Use periodic reconciliation whenever the source can hard-delete or move items"],"avoid":["Avoid timestamp-only detection when files are restored, renamed in bulk or touched by other tools","Avoid incremental runs after a parser, chunker or model change: rebuild instead","Avoid enabling soft-delete handling after the first run and expecting history to be corrected"],"flips":["Corpus is small and cheap to re-embed: a nightly full reload removes whole classes of bugs","Source offers a true change log (SQL change tracking or an all-versions change feed): prefer it to timestamps","Pipeline code changed: schedule a full rebuild into a new index"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Signal or approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Timestamp or high-water mark","Reliable last-modified field","Items are restored, bulk-moved or touched","Misses deletes and silent moves"],["SQL integrated change tracking","Source is a table and deletes matter","Source is a view","Must be enabled on the database"],["Content hash gate","Noisy timestamps, cosmetic edits","Reading the item is expensive","Needs a hash store and normalisation rules"],["Reconciliation by id comparison","Hard deletes or moves possible","Billions of ids per run","Extra listing cost, periodic not instant"],["Full rebuild","Small corpus or pipeline change","Large corpus with few changes","Embedding cost and run time scale with the corpus"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A restored file never reappears in search","cause":"Restoring a soft-deleted blob does not change `LastModified`, so the indexer sees nothing new","fix":"Touch the blob or reset the document, and document the restore procedure"},{"symptom":"Reorganised ADLS folders are missing from answers","cause":"Renaming a directory does not update the timestamps of the blobs inside it","fix":"Reset the indexer or reprocess the moved folder after bulk moves"},{"symptom":"Deleted source items stay in the index","cause":"No soft-delete policy existed on the first indexer run, or the source hard-deletes","fix":"Add the policy and flag-then-delete, or reconcile ids on a schedule"},{"symptom":"Embedding bills spike after a storage migration","cause":"A migration tool rewrote timestamps so every file looks changed","fix":"Gate on a content hash before parse and embed"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t06-s1","title":"Two million files, 0.1 percent daily churn","situation":"A knowledge base holds two million files and about 0.1 percent change each day. Embedding spend must follow the change rate, not the corpus size.","constraints":["Large corpus, low churn","Cost must scale with changes","Files live in Blob Storage","Daily freshness is fine"],"unknowns":["Do files get restored or renamed in bulk?","Do deletes need to propagate?"],"question":"How do you keep the index current?","options":[{"name":"Indexer change detection with a soft-delete policy, plus a weekly id reconciliation","verdict":"best","why":"Timestamps keep daily cost proportional to changes and reconciliation catches moves and hard deletes.","tradeoff":"You maintain the soft-delete convention and the reconciliation job."},{"name":"Nightly full rebuild","verdict":"poor","why":"It re-embeds 2 million files to pick up about 2,000 changes.","tradeoff":"Right for a small corpus or after a pipeline change."},{"name":"Timestamps only, no deletes handling","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest to run and covers edits.","tradeoff":"Deleted, restored and moved files drift out of sync."}],"flips":[{"if":"Corpus shrinks to a few thousand files","then":"Full reload is simpler."},{"if":"A migration rewrites timestamps","then":"Add a content hash gate before embedding."}],"takeaway":"Let cost follow churn, then add a safety net for what timestamps cannot see."},{"id":"w03-t06-s2","title":"Noisy timestamps after a migration","situation":"A storage migration touched half a million files without editing them. The next incremental run would re-embed all of them. Budget is limited and the content is unchanged.","constraints":["Timestamps are unreliable","Content did not change","Limited embedding budget","Index must stay online"],"unknowns":["Is a hash of each file already stored?","How expensive is reading a file compared with embedding it?"],"question":"What do you do before the run?","options":[{"name":"Compare content hashes and skip files whose hash matches the stored one","verdict":"best","why":"Reading and hashing is far cheaper than parsing and embedding, and unchanged files are skipped.","tradeoff":"You need stored hashes and stable normalisation."},{"name":"Let the indexer re-process everything","verdict":"good","why":"No new code and a clean result.","tradeoff":"Pays full embedding cost for no new information."},{"name":"Disable change detection and rebuild monthly","verdict":"poor","why":"It trades one cost for a month of staleness and loses delete handling.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a static archive."}],"flips":[{"if":"No hashes were ever stored","then":"Compute them once during this run, then use them from now on."},{"if":"Embedding is nearly free","then":"Re-processing is acceptable."}],"takeaway":"A hash gate turns noisy signals into correct decisions."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t06-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which Azure SQL change detection option is recommended for tables and also detects deleted rows?","options":["A high-water-mark column","`SqlIntegratedChangeTrackingPolicy`","A nightly full reload","A `LastModified` metadata field"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integrated change tracking works on tables and sees deletes. A high-water mark works for views but needs a soft-delete column. Full reload and `LastModified` are not SQL indexer policies.","ref":"tr5"},{"id":"w03-t06-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A corpus has 2,000,000 files, 0.1 percent change per day and about 12 chunks per file. How many chunks are embedded per day with perfect incremental ingestion?","answer":24000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"chunks","explain":"0.1 percent of 2,000,000 is 2,000 files. Multiplied by 12 chunks gives 24,000 chunks per day, compared with 24,000,000 for a full rebuild.","ref":"tr4"},{"id":"w03-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which situations leave the Blob indexer unaware of a change?","options":["A soft-deleted blob is restored","An ADLS Gen2 directory is renamed","A blob body is edited and `LastModified` updates","A blob is hard-deleted with no soft-delete policy"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Restores and directory renames do not change `LastModified`, and hard deletes are invisible without a policy. A normal edit updates `LastModified` and is detected.","ref":"tr4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is a content hash a useful gate in incremental ingestion?","back":"It skips items that were touched but not changed, so parse and embedding cost follow real edits, not timestamps."},{"front":"SQL indexer: integrated change tracking versus high-water mark?","back":"Change tracking: tables only, sees deletes, recommended. High-water mark: rowversion or last-updated column, works for views, needs a soft-delete column for deletes."},{"front":"Which indexer reset is GA, and which are preview?","back":"Reset Indexer is GA. Reset Documents, Reset Skills and Resync are preview APIs in `2026-08-01-preview`."}],"related":["w03-t02","w03-t07","w03-t08","w03-t09","w03-t10"],"refs":["tr4","tr5","tr6","tr9","tr11"]},{"id":"w03-t07","order":7,"group":"Keeping it fresh","title":"CDC and event-driven pipelines","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Change data capture and storage events push changes to you in seconds. Delivery is at least once and unordered, so the pipeline must treat events as hints and write idempotently.","useIf":"Freshness must be seconds, the source emits changes (change feed, database log, storage events) and you can run a queue and workers.","avoidIf":"Hourly freshness is enough and a supported indexer exists: events add operational burden with no business gain.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three event sources are common. The **Cosmos DB change feed** has two modes. Latest version mode records creates and updates, keeps only the newest change per item and does not capture deletes (use a soft-delete flag plus TTL). All versions and deletes mode records every create, update and delete in order, but it needs continuous backup, an opt-in flag, and can be read only within the backup retention window [[tr23]]. **Blob events** flow through Event Grid [[tr24]]. **Debezium** reads the database log and emits a delete event followed, for Kafka compaction, by a tombstone with a null value [[tr25]]."},{"t":"p","md":"None of them promises exactly once. Event Grid tries to deliver each event at least once, does not guarantee order, and retries on a schedule that starts at 10 seconds and backs off to every 12 hours up to 24 hours. The defaults are 30 attempts and a 1,440 minute time to live, and a failed event is dead-lettered only if you configured that [[tr26]]. Debezium can emit duplicates after a fault [[tr25]]. Items in one Cosmos DB transactional batch share a modification time and may arrive in any order [[tr23]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The practical answer is **effectively once**: at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent sink. Treat each event as a notification that item X changed. The worker re-reads the current state from the source, builds a stable key from the source id, and writes with `mergeOrUpload`. Re-reading makes stale and out-of-order events harmless, because the worker always indexes the latest state."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t07-d1","title":"Event as a hint, source as the truth","alt":"The source emits a change event to Event Grid, which delivers it to a worker. The worker reads current state from the source, upserts to the index with a stable key and acknowledges the event.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant S as Source\n  participant E as Event Grid\n  participant W as Worker\n  participant X as Index\n  S->>E: change event\n  E->>W: deliver, at least once\n  W->>S: read current state\n  W->>X: mergeOrUpload with stable key\n  W-->>E: acknowledge"},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Large blobs fire early","md":"To fire `BlobCreated` only after a block blob is fully committed, filter on the `CopyBlob`, `PutBlob`, `PutBlockList` or `FlushWithClose` calls [[tr24]]."}],"whereToUse":["Searchable tickets, chat or order data that must be fresh within seconds","Cosmos DB backed applications that need search updates driven by the change feed","Blob uploads that trigger parsing and indexing through Event Grid","Relational databases streamed through log-based CDC such as Debezium into a queue","Pipelines that need replay, dead-letter handling and per-item observability"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use events when the freshness target is seconds and the source can emit changes","Use all versions and deletes mode when hard deletes must be seen and continuous backup is available","Use re-read-on-event so ordering mistakes cannot overwrite new data with old","Use a dead-letter destination and alert on its depth"],"avoid":["Avoid assuming exactly-once delivery or global ordering","Avoid latest version change feed alone when deletes matter and you cannot add a soft-delete flag","Avoid running an event pipeline for a corpus that changes nightly"],"flips":["Freshness relaxes to 15 minutes or more: a GA indexer is cheaper to run","Hard deletes must be visible and continuous backup is off: add soft delete plus TTL or reconcile on a schedule","Events must be replayed after a bug: keep source state re-readable and key writes by stable id"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Scheduled indexer","Supported source, minutes of lag","Seconds of freshness, hard deletes","5 minute floor, delete blind spots"],["Cosmos DB change feed, latest version","Seconds, creates and updates only","Hard deletes matter","Only newest change per item, no deletes"],["Cosmos DB all versions and deletes","Every change and delete in order","No continuous backup, replay beyond retention","Opt-in, retention window, newer API surface"],["Storage events (Event Grid)","Files landing in Blob Storage","You need ordering","Duplicates, no order, early events on large uploads"],["Log-based CDC (Debezium)","Relational source, deletes and history","No platform team for Kafka-style infrastructure","At-least-once, connector operations"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Old data overwrites newer data in the index","cause":"Events arrived out of order and the worker indexed the event payload","fix":"Re-read current state from the source or compare a version number before writing"},{"symptom":"Duplicate chunks or double work after a failure","cause":"At-least-once delivery from Event Grid or Debezium with a random key per attempt","fix":"Derive keys from source id and position, and upsert"},{"symptom":"Deleted Cosmos DB items stay searchable","cause":"Latest version mode does not capture deletes","fix":"Use a soft-delete flag plus TTL, or all versions and deletes mode where the prerequisites hold"},{"symptom":"Events vanish after an outage","cause":"Retries ended at 30 attempts or 1,440 minutes with no dead-letter destination","fix":"Configure dead-lettering and a replay procedure"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t07-s1","title":"Hard deletes in a Cosmos DB catalogue","situation":"A product catalogue in Cosmos DB feeds search. The application hard-deletes retired products and the code cannot change this quarter. Continuous backup is enabled and the consumer keeps up within minutes.","constraints":["Hard deletes must reach the index","Seconds to minutes of freshness","Continuous backup enabled","App code frozen"],"unknowns":["How long is the continuous backup retention?","Can the consumer fall behind for days?"],"question":"How do you propagate deletes?","options":[{"name":"Read the change feed in all versions and deletes mode","verdict":"best","why":"It records deletes in order, and the prerequisites hold here.","tradeoff":"Opt-in feature, readable only within the backup retention window, and starts only from now or a checkpoint."},{"name":"Latest version mode plus a nightly id reconciliation","verdict":"good","why":"Works without extra prerequisites and catches deletes eventually.","tradeoff":"Deleted products stay searchable until the next reconciliation."},{"name":"Indexer on `_ts` with default settings","verdict":"poor","why":"Hard deletes are invisible to the indexer.","tradeoff":"Right when the app uses a Boolean soft-delete field."}],"flips":[{"if":"Continuous backup is not enabled","then":"Use latest version mode plus reconciliation, or change the app to soft delete."},{"if":"The app can be changed","then":"Soft delete plus TTL with the indexer is simplest."}],"takeaway":"Decide how deletes reach you before you choose the event source."},{"id":"w03-t07-s2","title":"Large uploads into Blob Storage","situation":"Users upload large files in many blocks. A worker is triggered per event. Sometimes it reads a half-written file or processes the same file twice.","constraints":["Large multi-block uploads","At-least-once delivery","Cost of parsing is high","Dead-letter is available"],"unknowns":["How often do duplicates happen?","What does the worker do on a partial read?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Filter events to completion calls, make the worker idempotent, and enable dead-lettering","verdict":"best","why":"It fixes early events at the source and makes duplicates harmless.","tradeoff":"A little setup and a stable-key convention."},{"name":"Add a fixed 10 minute delay before processing","verdict":"poor","why":"It guesses at upload time, still allows duplicates and delays every file.","tradeoff":"Only acceptable as a stop-gap."},{"name":"Retry the whole job whenever a read fails","verdict":"good","why":"Often works eventually.","tradeoff":"Repeats expensive parsing and does not stop duplicates."}],"flips":[{"if":"Files are tiny single-block uploads","then":"Early events are rare and idempotency alone suffices."},{"if":"Freshness relaxes to hours","then":"Switch to an indexer and drop the event path."}],"takeaway":"Fix early events at the filter and duplicates in the sink."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does Debezium guarantee about delivery?","options":["At least once, so duplicates can appear after a fault","Exactly once with global ordering","At most once","Ordered delivery across all tables"],"answer":0,"explain":"Debezium delivers at least once and may emit duplicates after a fault, so consumers should be idempotent. It does not promise exactly once or global ordering.","ref":"tr25"},{"id":"w03-t07-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which statements about Cosmos DB change feed modes are correct?","options":["Latest version mode does not capture deletes","All versions and deletes mode needs continuous backup and an opt-in feature flag","All versions and deletes mode can start from the beginning of time","Latest version mode keeps only the most recent change per item"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Latest version drops deletes and intermediate changes. All versions and deletes needs continuous backup and can start only from now or a checkpoint, within the retention window.","ref":"tr23"},{"id":"w03-t07-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Stale data keeps replacing fresh data in the index. Events are consumed in parallel. What is the most reliable fix?","options":["Use a bigger queue","Lower the retry count","Re-read current source state on each event, or compare a version before writing","Disable dead-lettering"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ordering is not guaranteed, so you must make order irrelevant by indexing current state or comparing versions. Queue size, retry count and dead-lettering do not fix ordering.","ref":"tr26"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Event Grid delivery guarantees and defaults?","back":"At least once, no ordering guarantee. Defaults: 30 attempts and 1,440 minute event TTL. Dead-letter only if configured."},{"front":"What is effectively-once processing?","back":"At-least-once delivery combined with an idempotent sink, such as a stable key and `mergeOrUpload`."},{"front":"Why re-read the source on each change event?","back":"Events can be duplicated or out of order. Indexing current state makes stale events harmless."}],"related":["w03-t01","w03-t06","w03-t08","w03-t10","w07-t15"],"refs":["tr23","tr24","tr25","tr26"]},{"id":"w03-t08","order":8,"group":"Keeping it fresh","title":"Idempotency, dedupe and hashing","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Idempotent ingestion gives the same index state however many times a change is delivered. It rests on stable ids, normalised content hashes and a deliberate rule for duplicates.","useIf":"Events, retries or replays can deliver the same document twice, or the same text exists in many copies.","avoidIf":"You build one-off loads that never rerun: key design still helps, but the dedupe machinery is overkill.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Delivery is at least once almost everywhere: Event Grid, Debezium and your own retries [[tr26]] [[tr25]]. The cure is at the sink. Push `mergeOrUpload` is the usual action for incremental updates, and `delete` on a missing key is safe, so a replayed batch is safe [[tr2]]. That only helps if the **key is stable**: build the document id from an immutable source id (a file id or primary key), never from a path that a rename changes, and never from a random value generated per attempt."},{"t":"p","md":"Chunk ids are a design choice. An ordinal id (`doc42-7`) is simple, but inserting a paragraph shifts every later chunk. An id based on the chunk's content hash stays stable for unchanged chunks, so you can reuse their embeddings and only embed the new ones. For per-document replacement, write the new set of chunks, then delete the chunks of that document that are not in the set. Index projections do part of this: projected keys carry a random hash that changes when the parent changes, so stale chunks are replaced, but children of a deleted parent are removed only when the data source has a deletion detection policy [[tr11]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Ids and hashes and what each is for","head":["Value","Built from","Used for","Trap"],"rows":[["Document id","Immutable source id","Stable upsert key","A path-based id orphans chunks on rename"],["Content hash","Normalised document text","Skip unchanged items","Unnormalised whitespace changes the hash"],["Chunk id","Document id plus ordinal or chunk hash","Per-chunk upsert and delete","Ordinals shift after an insert"],["Chunk hash","Normalised chunk text","Reuse stored embeddings","Must include the embedding model version"],["Near-duplicate signature","Shingles or embeddings","Find copies with small edits","A fuzzy match can merge different documents"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Normalise before hashing**: Unicode form, whitespace, line endings and volatile boilerplate such as a printed timestamp. **Dedupe with permissions in mind.** Two copies with identical text may carry different ACLs, and dropping one by hash can remove access or expose the wrong copy. Prefer tagging copies with a cluster id and collapsing them at query time, after security trimming."}],"whereToUse":["Any ingestion path with retries, queues or event delivery","Wikis and drives where pages are edited often and renamed or moved","Corpora with many copies of the same policy, template or email thread","Re-embedding jobs that should reuse vectors for unchanged chunks","Backfills that must be safe to restart after a crash"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use a stable key from an immutable source id for every document","Use `mergeOrUpload` so replays and retries land on the same document","Use a normalised content hash to skip unchanged items and detect duplicates","Use cluster ids, not deletion, when copies carry different permissions"],"avoid":["Avoid path-based ids when folders get renamed","Avoid random GUIDs minted per attempt, which turn every retry into a duplicate","Avoid fuzzy near-duplicate deletion without human or rule-based review"],"flips":["Documents rarely change and are small: ordinal chunk ids are fine, skip chunk-level hashing","Embedding cost dominates: add chunk-hash reuse so only new chunks are embedded","Copies carry different ACLs: keep every copy and collapse at query time"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Stable id plus upsert","Any pipeline with retries","Source has no immutable id","Needs a key convention agreed by all writers"],["Exact content hash","Byte or text identical copies","Copies differ by whitespace or a footer","Misses near-duplicates unless normalised"],["Near-duplicate clustering","Many lightly edited copies","Different documents look alike","False merges, tuning, compute"],["Chunk-hash embedding reuse","Large pages with small edits","Embedding model changed","A hash store and version in the hash input"],["No dedupe","Small corpus, copies are rare","Top-k fills with identical chunks","Wasted context and skewed answers"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Renaming a folder creates duplicate chunks","cause":"The document id was built from the path","fix":"Use an immutable source id and migrate existing keys once"},{"symptom":"A one-line edit re-embeds an entire long page","cause":"Chunk ids and embeddings depend on the ordinal position","fix":"Hash chunk text and reuse stored vectors for chunks whose hash and model version match"},{"symptom":"Unchanged files look changed after export","cause":"Whitespace, line endings or a printed date alter the hash","fix":"Normalise text and strip volatile boilerplate before hashing"},{"symptom":"Top results are five copies of one policy","cause":"No dedupe across departmental copies","fix":"Cluster copies, collapse after security trimming and keep each copy's ACL"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t08-s1","title":"Large wiki pages with frequent small edits","situation":"A wiki has pages of about 100 chunks. Editors change a sentence or add a paragraph near the top many times a day. Embedding spend is a concern and replays happen after failures.","constraints":["Small edits, long pages","Retries and replays occur","Embedding cost matters","Pages are renamed sometimes"],"unknowns":["Is an immutable page id available?","How often is the embedding model changed?"],"question":"How do you key and update chunks?","options":[{"name":"Immutable page id for documents, chunk ids from chunk hashes, replace per page and reuse unchanged vectors","verdict":"best","why":"Edits embed only new chunks, renames do not orphan data and replays are safe.","tradeoff":"A hash store, per-page replacement logic and the model version in the hash input."},{"name":"Ordinal chunk ids, re-embed the whole page on any edit","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and correct.","tradeoff":"Pays for about 100 embeddings on a one-word change."},{"name":"Random GUID per chunk per run","verdict":"poor","why":"Every retry or replay adds new copies.","tradeoff":"Only harmless for a single non-repeating load."}],"flips":[{"if":"Pages are short","then":"Ordinal ids with full re-embed cost little."},{"if":"The embedding model changes","then":"Cached vectors are invalid, so rebuild into a new index."}],"takeaway":"Stable ids make replays safe, and chunk hashes make edits cheap."},{"id":"w03-t08-s2","title":"Twelve departmental copies of one policy","situation":"A shared drive holds identical or near-identical copies of a policy in twelve departments. Each copy has its own permissions. Answers keep citing five copies of the same text.","constraints":["Per-document permissions","Many near-identical copies","Top-k is crowded by copies","Wrongly hidden answers are unacceptable"],"unknowns":["Are the copies bit-identical or edited?","Is a user allowed to see a copy that sits in another department?"],"question":"How do you handle the duplicates?","options":[{"name":"Keep each copy with its own ACL, add a cluster id and collapse at query time after trimming","verdict":"best","why":"Callers see one result per cluster from copies they may read, and no permission is lost.","tradeoff":"More chunks stored and a collapse step in retrieval."},{"name":"Exact-hash dedupe within the same ACL scope only","verdict":"good","why":"Removes true duplicates safely inside one permission group.","tradeoff":"Cross-department copies still crowd results."},{"name":"Global dedupe by hash keeping the first copy","verdict":"poor","why":"Users who cannot read the kept copy lose the answer, or the wrong ACL governs it.","tradeoff":"Right only when all copies share one permission scope."}],"flips":[{"if":"Every copy has the same permissions","then":"Global dedupe by normalised hash is simple and safe."},{"if":"Copies differ materially","then":"Treat them as distinct documents and rely on reranking."}],"takeaway":"Dedupe is a retrieval and security decision, not only a storage one."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After a folder rename, search shows duplicate chunks for the same file. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Hybrid search is misconfigured","The semantic ranker was enabled","The embedding model changed","Document ids were built from the file path"],"answer":3,"explain":"A path-based id changes on rename, so the upsert creates a new document and the old one remains. The other options do not generate duplicate documents.","ref":"tr2"},{"id":"w03-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which steps belong before computing a content hash used for change detection?","options":["Normalise Unicode and whitespace","Strip volatile boilerplate such as a printed timestamp","Add a random salt per run","Use the same normalisation in every pipeline version"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Hashes only help if equal content gives equal hashes, so normalise, strip volatility and keep rules stable. A random salt makes every hash differ.","ref":"tr2"},{"id":"w03-t08-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Two copies of a document have identical text but different ACLs. What is the safest dedupe approach?","options":["Delete the second copy by hash","Merge both ACLs into the first copy","Keep both with a cluster id and collapse at query time after security trimming","Drop the ACL fields to save space"],"answer":2,"explain":"Collapsing after trimming preserves each copy's permissions. Deleting or merging can hide answers or widen access, and dropping ACL fields breaks trimming.","ref":"tr11"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What makes a replayed push batch safe?","back":"`mergeOrUpload` with stable keys, and `delete` on a missing key being safe. The same batch lands on the same documents."},{"front":"Why use an immutable source id for the document key?","back":"Paths change on rename or move, which would orphan old chunks and create duplicates."},{"front":"Why include the embedding model version in a chunk-hash cache key?","back":"A stored vector is only valid for its model. A different model needs new vectors even for identical text."}],"related":["w03-t06","w03-t07","w03-t09","w03-t10","w07-t15"],"refs":["tr2","tr11","tr25","tr26"]},{"id":"w03-t09","order":9,"group":"Keeping it fresh","title":"Deletes, versioning and re-embedding","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Deletes need a visible tombstone, versions need a rule for what the index keeps, and re-embedding needs a second index and an alias, because vectors from different models cannot be mixed.","useIf":"Content is removed or superseded, a legal deletion deadline exists, or you will change embedding models during the life of the index.","avoidIf":"The corpus is a frozen archive that is never edited or re-embedded: a simple one-time build is enough.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Deletes.** An indexer cannot see what the source silently removes. Blob has two soft-delete strategies: native blob soft delete (Blob Storage only, blob versioning must be off, retention well above the indexer interval) or a custom metadata flag such as `IsDeleted=true` followed by a physical delete. The policy must exist from the first run [[tr4]]. SQL uses `SoftDeleteColumnDeletionDetectionPolicy` [[tr5]] and Cosmos DB needs a top-level Boolean field [[tr6]]. Child chunks of a deleted parent are removed only when the data source has a deletion detection policy [[tr11]]. A reconciliation job is your proof that deletes happened."},{"t":"p","md":"**Versioning.** Decide whether the index holds only the latest version of a document or also history. Latest-only is simplest: replace on change. History needs fields such as `version` and `is_current` and a filter, and it raises ACL and storage questions."},{"t":"p","md":"**Re-embedding.** You cannot upgrade an embedding model in place, and vectors from different models are not comparable [[tr33]]. The query-time vectorizer must use the same model as the embedding skill [[tr12]]. So build a second index with the new model, keep both live while you validate, then repoint an **alias**, a service-level name that is separate from indexes [[tr10]]. Keep the old index through a rollback window. HNSW vectors consume memory quota, so two live copies roughly double that need [[tr41]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t09-d1","title":"Blue-green re-embedding behind an alias","alt":"Applications query an alias that points at index v1. A backfill and dual-write fill index v2 with the new model, a quality gate checks it, and then the alias is repointed from v1 to v2.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[App queries] --> L[Alias docs-live]\n  L --> V1[(Index v1 old model)]\n  B[Backfill and dual-write] --> V2[(Index v2 new model)]\n  V2 --> Q[Quality gate]\n  Q --> C[Repoint alias]\n  C --> L"},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Check retirement dates live","md":"The retirement schedule page (updated 2026-09-21) lists text-embedding-3-large, 3-small and ada-002 as retiring 2028-02-09. An older page said ada-002 retired no earlier than April 30, 2026. Check the live schedule before quoting a date [[tr32]]."}],"whereToUse":["Compliance-driven removal of documents within a stated deadline","Knowledge bases where policies are superseded and old versions must not be answered from","Changing the embedding model, dimensions or chunking strategy of a live index","Planning capacity for the period when two indexes are live","Release processes that need a tested rollback"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use soft-delete flags or native soft delete with a policy set before the first run","Use blue-green with an alias for any user-facing re-embedding","Use dual-write or a checkpointed replay so changes made during the backfill reach the new index","Keep the old index through a rollback window and quality checks"],"avoid":["Avoid relying on physical deletes in the source for indexers that cannot detect them","Avoid re-embedding in place: there is no mixed-model state that works","Avoid deleting the old index before the new one has passed evaluation"],"flips":["Downtime is acceptable and the corpus is small: rebuild in place during a maintenance window","Memory headroom is tight: stage the cutover by partition or add capacity for the overlap, because two live HNSW copies roughly double the memory need","Retirement date approaches: schedule the migration with margin, not at the deadline"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Re-embedding approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["New index behind an alias (blue-green)","User-facing, rollback needed","Memory or cost cannot carry two copies","Double vector storage during overlap"],["Second vector field in the same index","Gradual cutover by query, same filters","Index is near its memory quota","Both vector sets held in one index"],["Rebuild in place in a maintenance window","Small corpus, downtime accepted","Users need continuity or rollback","Downtime and no safe fallback"],["Soft delete then purge (deletes)","Source can express a flag","Source hard-deletes outside your control","Convention must exist from the first run"],["Reconciliation by id comparison (deletes)","Any source","Huge id sets, instant deletion needed","Periodic, extra listing cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Search returns nonsense after the model change","cause":"The query vectorizer still uses the old model while the index holds new-model vectors","fix":"Change the vectorizer with the index and test with known queries before the alias moves"},{"symptom":"Deleted parent documents leave orphan chunks","cause":"Projected child chunks are removed only if the data source has a deletion detection policy","fix":"Add the policy and verify with a reconciliation count"},{"symptom":"No way back after a bad cutover","cause":"The old index was dropped right after the alias moved","fix":"Keep it through a rollback window, then delete"},{"symptom":"The build of the new index fails on capacity","cause":"HNSW vectors use memory quota and two copies double the need","fix":"Check vector index size before starting and plan capacity for both"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t09-s1","title":"Model migration for a customer-facing index","situation":"A production assistant uses an index of 20 million chunks. A new embedding model improves retrieval on the evaluation set. The assistant cannot go down and a quick rollback must exist.","constraints":["No downtime","Rollback required","Memory headroom exists for two copies","Evaluation set available"],"unknowns":["How many changes arrive during the backfill?","How long is the overlap affordable?"],"question":"How do you migrate?","options":[{"name":"New index with the new model behind an alias, dual-write during backfill, quality gate, then repoint","verdict":"best","why":"Users keep the old index until the new one passes evaluation, and rollback is a repoint.","tradeoff":"Double vector storage and a dual-write path for a while."},{"name":"Add a second vector field to the same index and switch queries by flag","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps one index and one set of filters, with gradual cutover.","tradeoff":"One index carries both vector sets, so memory and cleanup are harder to reason about."},{"name":"Rebuild in place during a weekend window","verdict":"poor","why":"It takes the service down and leaves no safe fallback if quality regresses.","tradeoff":"Right for a small internal tool that tolerates downtime."}],"flips":[{"if":"Memory only fits one copy","then":"Stage the migration by partition or add capacity for the overlap."},{"if":"The corpus is a few thousand chunks","then":"In-place rebuild is acceptable."}],"takeaway":"Treat a model change like a database migration with a cutover and a way back."},{"id":"w03-t09-s2","title":"A 24 hour removal deadline from Blob Storage","situation":"Documents stored in Blob Storage must disappear from search within 24 hours of a removal request. The indexer runs every hour. Blob versioning is enabled for other reasons.","constraints":["24 hour deletion deadline","Hourly indexer","Blob versioning is on","Audit wants proof"],"unknowns":["Who sets the flag and how is it audited?","Are there copies in caches or exports?"],"question":"How do you implement deletes?","options":[{"name":"Custom metadata flag such as `IsDeleted=true`, indexer deletion policy, physical delete after the run, plus reconciliation","verdict":"best","why":"It works with versioning on, is visible to the indexer and leaves an audit trail.","tradeoff":"The convention must be enforced and the physical delete must wait for the indexer."},{"name":"Native blob soft delete","verdict":"poor","why":"Native soft delete needs blob versioning off, which is not the case here.","tradeoff":"Right when versioning is off and retention exceeds the indexer interval."},{"name":"Delete blobs directly and wait","verdict":"poor","why":"The indexer cannot see hard deletes by default, so entries stay in the index.","tradeoff":"Right only with a reconciliation job that removes the orphans."}],"flips":[{"if":"Versioning can be turned off","then":"Native soft delete with long retention is simpler."},{"if":"Deadline shrinks to minutes","then":"Delete from the index directly through the push API as part of the removal workflow."}],"takeaway":"Pick the delete signal that your source and settings allow, and prove it with reconciliation."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does an index alias let you do during re-embedding?","options":["Mix old and new vectors in one field","Upgrade the embedding model in place","Skip the quality check","Repoint applications to a rebuilt index without code changes"],"answer":3,"explain":"An alias is a service-level name you repoint to cut over. Mixing models in one field is invalid, in-place upgrades are not supported, and quality checks remain necessary.","ref":"tr10"},{"id":"w03-t09-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Your live index uses 40 GB of vector index quota. You build an equal-size index with a new model and keep both live. What is the minimum vector quota you need, in GB?","answer":80,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"GB","explain":"Two equal indexes need about 80 GB because HNSW graphs are held in memory. Real needs vary with the new model's dimensions and compression.","ref":"tr41"},{"id":"w03-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which are true for blob delete handling in Azure AI Search?","options":["Deletes are not tracked by default","Native soft delete requires blob versioning to be off","The policy can be added any time and corrects history","A custom metadata flag followed by a physical delete is supported"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Defaults ignore deletes, native soft delete needs versioning off, and a metadata flag is a supported strategy. The policy must exist from the first run, so it cannot correct history.","ref":"tr4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why can't you upgrade an embedding model in place?","back":"Vectors from different models are not comparable. Azure says you need new embeddings, so build a new index and switch an alias."},{"front":"Two soft-delete strategies for the Blob indexer?","back":"Native blob soft delete (versioning off, long retention) or a metadata flag like `IsDeleted=true` then physical delete. Policy must exist from the first run."},{"front":"What must match between the embedding skill and the index at query time?","back":"The vectorizer's model must be the same as the embedding skill's model that produced the stored vectors."}],"related":["w03-t06","w03-t08","w03-t10","w04-t12","w04-t15"],"refs":["tr4","tr5","tr6","tr10","tr11","tr12","tr32","tr33","tr41"]},{"id":"w03-t10","order":10,"group":"Quality and failure","title":"Failure handling: dead letters, backpressure and replay","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Ingestion fails in three ways: transient errors you retry, poison items you quarantine, and overload you absorb with backpressure. A dead-letter queue and idempotent replay make all three recoverable.","useIf":"Your pipeline calls rate-limited services (embeddings, parsers, source APIs) or processes untrusted files at volume.","avoidIf":"A tiny batch job runs under supervision: log failures and rerun it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Classify a failure before you react. **Transient** (429, 503, a timeout): retry with exponential backoff and jitter. **Permanent or poison** (a corrupt PDF, an unsupported type): stop retrying and quarantine. **Partial** (some items in a batch failed): push returns HTTP 207, so read the per-document status and retry only the failed keys, while 503 means wait and retry [[tr2]]. Indexers expose `maxFailedItems` and `maxFailedItemsPerBatch`: a positive integer tolerates that many failures and `-1` continues no matter how many errors occur [[tr8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"A **dead-letter queue** holds what could not be processed. Service Bus gives each queue a DLQ sub-queue. A message moves there when its delivery count passes the limit (default 10, reason `MaxDeliveryCountExceeded`), and nothing cleans it up automatically, so you need alerts on depth and age [[tr27]]. Event Grid dead-letters only if you configure a destination, and it drops events on errors such as 400, 413 and 403 when you do not [[tr26]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Backpressure** protects the slowest stage, usually the embedding call. Azure OpenAI allows 2,048 inputs and 300,000 tokens per embeddings request, quota is scoped to the subscription, and 429 can appear even when usage metrics look below quota [[tr28]]. Use a bounded queue, a shared concurrency limiter and a token-bucket on embedding calls, honour `Retry-After` where given [[tr29]], and pause intake when the limiter is saturated. **Replay** is safe only if writes are idempotent (see the idempotency topic). After a fix, move DLQ messages back to the main queue. For indexers, Reset Indexer is GA and Reset Documents is preview [[tr9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t10-d1","title":"Retry, quarantine and replay loop","alt":"A worker takes items from a queue and calls the embedding service then the index. Transient failures go to a retry with backoff and back to the queue. Permanent failures or too many attempts go to a dead-letter queue, then are fixed and replayed to the queue.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Queue] --> W[Worker]\n  W --> E[Embed call]\n  E --> P[Push to index]\n  W -->|transient| R[Retry with backoff]\n  R --> Q\n  W -->|permanent or max tries| D[Dead-letter queue]\n  D --> F[Fix and replay]\n  F --> Q"}],"whereToUse":["Event-driven pipelines where a single corrupt file can block a queue","Backfills and re-embedding runs that must stay inside embedding quota","Crawlers of rate-limited APIs such as Graph and SharePoint","Indexer configurations that need an error budget, not all-or-nothing runs","Operations runbooks that define who inspects and replays dead letters"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use retries with backoff and jitter for transient errors only","Use a DLQ with alerts on depth and age for poison items","Use a shared limiter and bounded queue to keep embedding calls inside quota","Use idempotent writes so replay is safe"],"avoid":["Avoid infinite retry loops, which let one bad item hold the queue","Avoid `maxFailedItems` of `-1` unless you alert on error counts separately","Avoid unbounded parallelism against a quota-limited API"],"flips":["Volume is small and supervised: log failures and rerun, skip the DLQ","Latency matters more than completeness for a source: drop low-value failures after a warning, with a counter","Quota is subscription-wide and shared with other workloads: add a central limiter, not per-worker limits"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Mechanism","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Retry with backoff and jitter","Transient 429, 503, timeouts","Poison items that never succeed","Latency; retry storms if uncapped"],["Dead-letter queue and replay","Poison items, bugs fixed later","Nobody watches the DLQ","No automatic cleanup, needs alerts and a runbook"],["Indexer error budget (`maxFailedItems`)","Pull indexers over messy files","You need every item or nothing","`-1` hides systematic failure"],["Backpressure and rate limiting","Embedding or API quota is the bottleneck","Throughput must be maximal and quota is ample","Slower intake, limiter tuning"],["Skip and log","Low-value, optional content","Every document matters (legal, support)","Silent data gaps"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The whole queue stalls on one file","cause":"A poison item is retried forever and blocks consumers","fix":"Cap attempts, dead-letter with a reason and move on"},{"symptom":"429 errors multiply under load","cause":"Workers retry immediately and in parallel, so throttled calls count against limits","fix":"Back off with jitter, honour `Retry-After` and share one limiter"},{"symptom":"The DLQ holds thousands of messages nobody reads","cause":"Service Bus never cleans the DLQ automatically and no alert was set","fix":"Alert on DLQ depth and oldest age and assign an owner"},{"symptom":"An indexer reports success while documents are missing","cause":"`maxFailedItems` is `-1`, so errors never fail the run","fix":"Set a finite budget and alert on the error count per run"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t10-s1","title":"Five million chunks against an embedding quota","situation":"A re-embedding backfill must finish in a week. The embedding deployment shares quota with a live assistant that must not be starved. Occasional 429 responses appear.","constraints":["Shared subscription quota","Live traffic must stay healthy","One week to finish","Checkpointed, idempotent writes"],"unknowns":["What share of quota can the backfill use?","Are there quiet hours?"],"question":"How do you drive the backfill?","options":[{"name":"Bounded workers behind a shared token-bucket limiter, honour `Retry-After`, checkpoint progress, run harder in quiet hours","verdict":"best","why":"It uses spare quota without starving the live assistant and survives restarts.","tradeoff":"Slower than flat-out and the limiter needs tuning."},{"name":"One sequential worker","verdict":"good","why":"Safe and simple.","tradeoff":"May miss the one week target at five million chunks."},{"name":"Maximum parallelism with immediate retries","verdict":"poor","why":"It triggers 429 storms and can starve production traffic.","tradeoff":"Right only on a dedicated deployment that nothing else uses."}],"flips":[{"if":"A separate deployment is available for the backfill","then":"Run it harder, limited only by that quota."},{"if":"The deadline is tight","then":"Request more quota or use a batch-style option before raising concurrency."}],"takeaway":"Treat quota as a shared resource and meter your own consumption."},{"id":"w03-t10-s2","title":"One poison file in every few thousand","situation":"An event-driven pipeline handles support attachments. Roughly one in a few thousand files crashes the parser. These files sometimes matter for compliance.","constraints":["Occasional poison files","Some files matter legally","Queue with delivery counts","Team on call during business hours"],"unknowns":["Is the parser failure deterministic?","Who owns the DLQ?"],"question":"What do you do with the failing files?","options":[{"name":"Dead-letter after the max delivery count, alert on depth, fix the parser, replay","verdict":"best","why":"The queue keeps flowing, nothing is lost and replay is safe with idempotent writes.","tradeoff":"Someone must own the DLQ and the runbook."},{"name":"Skip the file and write a warning log","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and keeps flow.","tradeoff":"Risks silent gaps for compliance files unless the log is monitored."},{"name":"Retry until it works","verdict":"poor","why":"A deterministic failure never succeeds and blocks consumers.","tradeoff":"Right for transient errors with capped attempts."}],"flips":[{"if":"The content is optional and low value","then":"Skip and log with a counter is enough."},{"if":"A parser upgrade fixes the class of failure","then":"Replay the whole DLQ after the upgrade."}],"takeaway":"Quarantine what you cannot process, and make replay a routine."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"You embed 10,000 chunks of 500 tokens each. Limits per request are 2,048 inputs and 300,000 tokens in total. What is the minimum number of requests?","answer":17,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","explain":"The token limit binds first: 300,000 / 500 = 600 inputs per request. 10,000 / 600 = 16.7, so 17 requests. The 2,048 input limit alone would suggest 5.","ref":"tr28"},{"id":"w03-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An indexer run succeeds but many documents are missing. `maxFailedItems` is `-1`. What does that setting do?","options":["Fails the run on the first error","Retries each item forever","Skips unsupported content types only","Continues no matter how many errors occur"],"answer":3,"explain":"A positive integer is the number of tolerated failures, and `-1` continues regardless of errors, which hides problems. It does not fail on the first error or retry forever.","ref":"tr8"},{"id":"w03-t10-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are good practices for a DLQ in an ingestion pipeline?","options":["Alert on depth and oldest message age","Record the failure reason with each message","Rely on automatic cleanup","Make writes idempotent so replay is safe"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Alerts, failure reasons and idempotent writes make dead letters actionable. Service Bus does not clean the DLQ automatically, so cleanup is your job.","ref":"tr27"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Service Bus DLQ default max delivery count and reason code?","back":"Default 10. The reason is `MaxDeliveryCountExceeded`. Nothing cleans the DLQ automatically."},{"front":"Embeddings request limits on Azure OpenAI?","back":"2,048 inputs and 300,000 tokens per request, summed across inputs. Compute both limits when batching."},{"front":"Why can a 429 appear below your token quota?","back":"Bursts can exceed rate limits even when usage metrics look fine. Smooth traffic with a limiter and backoff."}],"related":["w03-t01","w03-t07","w03-t08","w16-t04","w16-t08"],"refs":["tr2","tr8","tr9","tr26","tr27","tr28","tr29"]},{"id":"w03-t11","order":11,"group":"Quality and failure","title":"Quality gates: PII, ACL capture and metadata","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Quality gates sit between parsing and indexing. They stop bad text, sensitive data and chunks with no permissions from entering the index, and they stamp each chunk with the metadata that retrieval and debugging need.","useIf":"The index holds personal data, per-document permissions or content you must trace back to a source, version and parser.","avoidIf":"The corpus is public, flat and low risk: keep the gates light and measure drift instead.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**PII before embedding.** Once personal data is turned into a vector and written to an index, removing it means rebuilding. The PII Detection skill returns masked text when `maskingMode` is `replace` (the default is `none`), with `maskingCharacter` defaulting to `*`. Other parameters are `minimumPrecision` (0.0 to 1.0, default 0.0, so low-confidence matches are included until you raise it), `domain` (`phi` or `none`) and `piiCategories` [[tr15]]. Azure AI Language PII has text, conversation and document-based modes, and no detector guarantees full coverage, so test your own formats [[tr40]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**ACLs at ingest.** Permissions must be captured when content is read and inherited by every chunk. Index projection mappings copy ACL fields such as `metadata_user_ids`, `metadata_group_ids` and `metadata_spo_site_url` onto each chunk [[tr14]]. Knowledge source `ingestionPermissionOptions` (for example `userIds`, `groupIds`) needs preview API `2026-08-01-preview`, and `2026-04-01` does not support it. ADLS Gen2 POSIX-like ACL and RBAC scope ingestion, SharePoint ACL ingestion and Purview label ingestion are previews [[tr14]] [[tr7]]. Security trimming itself belongs to the access-control chapter."},{"t":"p","md":"**Metadata and observability.** Stamp each chunk with source id, path, page or region, language, version, content hash, parser version, embedding model version and a sensitivity label. Then count items at every stage, measure lag from source change to searchable, watch dead-letter depth and run periodic reconciliation to detect drift between source and index. These practices are not vendor settings: define the thresholds from your own corpus."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w03-t11-d1","title":"Gates between parser and index","alt":"Parsed text passes a text quality gate, a PII policy gate and an ACL present gate, then gets metadata and is indexed. A failure at any gate sends the item to quarantine.","src":"flowchart LR\n  P[Parsed text] --> G1{Text quality}\n  G1 --> G2{PII policy}\n  G2 --> G3{ACL present}\n  G3 --> M[Add metadata]\n  M --> I[(Index)]\n  G1 -->|fail| X[Quarantine]\n  G2 -->|fail| X\n  G3 -->|fail| X"}],"whereToUse":["Support, HR and health content where personal data must not reach the index","Multi-department corpora where chunks inherit file or site permissions","Regulated corpora that need lineage from answer to source, version and parser","Large pipelines where drift between source and index must be measured","Any index that will later be filtered by language, type, date or sensitivity"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Mask or exclude PII before chunks are embedded, when answers do not need the value","Fail closed: quarantine any chunk that arrives without ACL fields","Store lineage metadata on every chunk, including parser and model versions","Track stage counts, lag and drift as ingestion metrics with alerts"],"avoid":["Avoid redacting after embedding: vectors and stored text already carry the data","Avoid default ACLs such as everyone-can-read as a fallback","Avoid treating one PII detector as complete: it can miss formats and languages"],"flips":["Answers must quote the sensitive value: keep it, encrypt and restrict access, and justify the legal basis","ACL ingestion for your source is preview and policy forbids preview: capture ACLs with your own reader and push them as fields","Permission churn is fast: add a re-sync path or a live source check, see the ACL sync topic"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Redact PII before embedding","Answers never need the value","Users must read the real value","Detector misses leak through, masks hide context"],["Tag PII and filter at query time","Some callers may see it","Index must hold no PII at all","PII sits in the index and vectors"],["Exclude documents containing PII","Low-value or risky documents","Documents are essential","Coverage gaps"],["Capture ACLs at ingest on each chunk","Group-based, slowly changing permissions","Revocation must be instant","Sync lag, preview APIs for some sources"],["Query-time check against the source","Fast-changing, per-document rights","High query volume","Latency and source load"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Users see documents they cannot open in the source","cause":"Chunks were indexed without ACL fields, or the projection did not copy parent ACL fields","fix":"Map ACL fields in the index projection and quarantine any chunk with empty ACLs"},{"symptom":"Personal data appears in answers after masking was added","cause":"Masking ran after embedding, or only on display text","fix":"Mask before chunking and embedding, then rebuild old vectors"},{"symptom":"Too much text is masked and answers lose context","cause":"`minimumPrecision` is at its default of 0.0, which keeps low-confidence matches","fix":"Raise `minimumPrecision`, restrict `piiCategories` and evaluate on labelled samples"},{"symptom":"A revoked user still gets results","cause":"ACLs were captured once and never re-synced","fix":"Add a permission re-sync path and drift checks, and shorten cache lifetimes"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w03-t11-s1","title":"Support transcripts with customer details","situation":"Chat transcripts hold names, emails and account numbers. The assistant summarises issues and never needs those values. A privacy review will inspect the index.","constraints":["Answers do not need the PII","Privacy review of the index","Transcripts are long","Batch ingestion nightly"],"unknowns":["Which languages and entity types appear?","Is there a labelled sample to measure misses?"],"question":"Where do you handle PII?","options":[{"name":"Detect and mask with the PII skill before chunking and embedding, with a tuned `minimumPrecision`","verdict":"best","why":"The index and the vectors never hold the values, so the review passes by design.","tradeoff":"Detector misses still leak and masks reduce context."},{"name":"Index raw text, tag PII and filter at query time","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps full text for authorised callers.","tradeoff":"The index and vectors still contain personal data."},{"name":"Keep raw text and rely on an output filter","verdict":"poor","why":"The data is stored, embedded and retrievable, and the filter is the only barrier.","tradeoff":"Right only when callers need the values and access is tightly controlled."}],"flips":[{"if":"Agents must quote account numbers","then":"Keep them in a restricted field with access control and logging."},{"if":"The detector misses your custom ID format","then":"Add custom rules and re-test before launch."}],"takeaway":"Remove what you do not need before it becomes a vector."},{"id":"w03-t11-s2","title":"ADLS folders with POSIX-style permissions","situation":"Documents sit in ADLS Gen2 folders with different permissions per team. Production starts in six weeks. Policy forbids preview features in production.","constraints":["Per-folder permissions","No preview features","Group-based, changes weekly","Revocation within a day is acceptable"],"unknowns":["How many groups does a user belong to?","Can the reader access the folder ACLs?"],"question":"How do you capture permissions?","options":[{"name":"Read ACLs with your own reader, write group id fields on every chunk through the push API, and re-sync daily","verdict":"best","why":"It stays on GA surfaces, makes ACLs part of each chunk and meets the one day revocation target.","tradeoff":"You build and run the reader and the sync."},{"name":"Use the preview ACL ingestion for ADLS Gen2","verdict":"good","why":"Least code and native behaviour.","tradeoff":"Breaks the no-preview rule and the API may change."},{"name":"Index everything and filter by folder name in the application","verdict":"poor","why":"Folder names are not permissions and the filter is easy to bypass or get wrong.","tradeoff":"Right never as a security boundary, only as a convenience facet."}],"flips":[{"if":"Policy allows preview","then":"The native ADLS ACL ingestion cuts effort."},{"if":"Revocation must be immediate","then":"Add a live source check for sensitive hits."}],"takeaway":"Capture permissions at ingest on a supported surface, and plan their refresh."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w03-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the default `maskingMode` of the PII Detection skill?","options":["`replace`","`redact`","`none`","`hash`"],"answer":2,"explain":"The default is `none`, so you must set `replace` to get `maskedText`. `redact` and `hash` are not documented values.","ref":"tr15"},{"id":"w03-t11-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each PII skill parameter with its effect.","pairs":[{"l":"`minimumPrecision`","r":"Confidence threshold from 0.0 to 1.0, default 0.0"},{"l":"`maskingMode`","r":"Whether masked text is returned (`replace`) or not (`none`)"},{"l":"`maskingCharacter`","r":"The character used for masking, `*` by default"},{"l":"`piiCategories`","r":"Which entity categories to detect"}],"explain":"Each parameter controls one part of detection and masking. A default threshold of 0.0 keeps low-confidence matches, which causes over-masking.","ref":"tr15"},{"id":"w03-t11-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which are correct about ACL capture at ingest?","options":["Index projection mappings must copy ACL fields onto every chunk","`ingestionPermissionOptions` works on the `2026-04-01` stable API","ADLS Gen2 POSIX-like ACL ingestion is preview","A chunk with empty ACL fields should be quarantined"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Chunks must inherit ACL fields, ADLS ACL ingestion is preview and empty ACLs should fail closed. `ingestionPermissionOptions` requires `2026-08-01-preview`.","ref":"tr14"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why must PII masking happen before embedding?","back":"After embedding, the vector and stored text already carry the data. Removing it later means rebuilding the index."},{"front":"Which settings does ACL ingestion need in Azure AI Search knowledge sources?","back":"`ingestionPermissionOptions` (such as `userIds`, `groupIds`) on `2026-08-01-preview`, plus projection mappings copying ACL fields to every chunk."},{"front":"Name four metadata fields worth stamping on each chunk.","back":"Source id, page or region, content hash and parser or embedding model version. Also language, sensitivity label and chunk position."}],"related":["w03-t02","w03-t09","w03-t10","w06-t09","w07-t11","w07-t15"],"refs":["tr7","tr14","tr15","tr40"]}]});
